 The desktop shell frame on the real runtime -- H4's first rung. The
 guios desktop proved a 19-app shell in the emulator, but it leans on
 codex-vm devices no real machine has: a host-side triangle rasterizer
 at MMIO 0xBE000000 with doorbell ports 0x400-0x402, absolute mouse
 coordinates synthesized on ports 0xE1-0xE4, and frame pacing by
 port-224 VM exits. This chapter is the frame those apps will land on,
 built only from what the Option A runtime actually provides: the GOP
 framebuffer from the handoff cells, keyboard and mouse from the H2
 USB stack (or PS/2 through the same mailbox), the PIT tick cell for
 pacing, and the CMOS RTC for wall-clock time.

 There is no frame loop running at a made-up rate. The desktop is
 event-driven: the loop polls the keyboard mailbox and the mouse pump
 -- both allocation-free when idle -- and repaints exactly what an
 event dirtied. The clock redraws when the tick cell says a second
 has passed. A machine showing an idle desktop executes a poll loop
 that allocates nothing and writes nothing.

 We say:

Section: Palette and Layout

 Three of these are the Seattle Seahawks brand colours exactly, and the
 rest are a ramp of College Navy so nothing on the desktop is a blue that
 belongs to nobody. The exact ones are the bar (College Navy 002244), the
 body text (Wolf Grey A5ACAF) and the hint line (Action Green 69BE28).

  dk-col-bar : Integer = #002244
  dk-col-wall : Integer = #001428
  dk-col-task : Integer = #000E1C
  dk-col-win : Integer = #003366
  dk-col-title : Integer = #004488
  dk-col-text : Integer = #A5ACAF
  dk-col-bright : Integer = #FFFFFF
  dk-col-hint : Integer = #69BE28
  dk-col-warn : Integer = #FFB040

  dk-bar-h : Integer = 22
  dk-task-h : Integer = 28
  dk-win-w : Integer = 360
  dk-title-h : Integer = 20
  dk-body-y0 : Integer = 32
  dk-line-h : Integer = 18
  dk-para-gap : Integer = 6

  dk-win-pad-b : Integer = 30

  dk-palette : Palette
  dk-palette = Palette {
    pal-bg = dk-col-wall,
    pal-fg = dk-col-text,
    pal-primary = dk-col-hint,
    pal-secondary = dk-col-title,
    pal-accent = dk-col-bright,
    pal-muted = dk-col-task,
    pal-error = dk-col-warn,
    pal-success = dk-col-hint,
    pal-warning = dk-col-warn,
    pal-border = dk-col-bar
  }

Section: Appearance

 Two `ds` cells hold what the desktop looks like: a scheme index and a bitmask
 of adornments. They are cells rather than a record because every pane already
 reads its state out of `ds` with `peek-32`, so no pane signature had to grow a
 second parameter to carry a theme. Cell 48 is `desk-gpu-cell` and is not free.

  dk-scheme-cell : Integer = 52
  dk-adorn-cell : Integer = 56
  dk-sound-cell : Integer = 64
  dk-sound-on-cell : Integer = 68
  dk-icons-cell : Integer = 72

  dk-adorn-border : Integer = 1
  dk-adorn-gradient : Integer = 2
  dk-adorn-shadow : Integer = 4
  dk-adorn-round : Integer = 8

 All four adornments on. This was 11, which is border, gradient and round
 with SHADOW left off, so the one adornment that makes a control look like
 it sits above the ground was the one nobody saw. It was never unreachable:
 the Appearance pane has always offered it and a user could always turn it
 on, which is also why turning it on by default is safe rather than a
 decision taken away from them. `s` in that pane puts it back.

 Looked at before it was flipped, at 1600x900 with a face on the ESP: what
 `shadow-subtle` draws here is a hard two pixel offset rectangle and not a
 blur, because this path has no alpha and says so, and at that size it
 reads as an edge under the control rather than as a smear.

  dk-adorn-default : Integer = 15

  dk-adorn-on : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dk-adorn-on (flags) (bit) = bit-and flags bit /= 0

  dk-on-off : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dk-on-off (flags) (bit) = if dk-adorn-on flags bit then "on" else "off"

  dk-adorn-flip : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-adorn-flip (flags) (bit) = if dk-adorn-on flags bit then flags - bit else flags + bit

 Schemes 0 to 3 are PALETTES over the desk's own surface style, and 4
 upward are whole design languages from `GopStyleKit` -- corners, relief,
 air and outline as well as colour. That is the difference between "lcars
 orange" and LCARS, and it is why the two kinds share one list: a person
 choosing an appearance is choosing one thing, not a colour and then a
 shape.

  dk-scheme-count : Integer = 6

  dk-lang : Integer -> DesignLanguage
  dk-lang (i) = if i == 4 then sk-flightdeck else sk-chevy

  dk-is-lang : Integer -> Boolean
  dk-is-lang (i) = i >= 4

  dk-scheme-name : Integer -> Text
  dk-scheme-name (i) =
    if dk-is-lang i then (dk-lang i).dl-name
    else if i == 1 then "terminal" else if i == 2 then "lcars" else if i == 3 then "minimal" else "seahawk"

  dk-scheme-palette : Integer -> Palette
  dk-scheme-palette (i) =
    if dk-is-lang i then (dk-lang i).dl-palette
    else if i == 1 then palette-terminal else if i == 2 then palette-lcars else if i == 3 then palette-minimal else dk-palette

Section: Appearance That Survives A Reboot

 The two cells above are the whole of what the Appearance pane edits, and
 until now the whole of what it lost: `desk-run` poked the default into
 `dk-adorn-cell` on every boot, so a user could pick a scheme, look at it,
 and find it gone the next time the machine came up.

 The cells stay the read path. A paint reads them with `peek-32` and costs
 nothing, and putting a keyed store on the frame budget to save two
 integers would be the machinery exceeding the cost. The store is touched
 exactly twice: once at boot, and once per keystroke that changes
 something.

  dk-set-scheme : Integer = 1
  dk-set-adorn : Integer = 2
  dk-set-sound : Integer = 3

  dk-settings-keys : List SettingKey
  dk-settings-keys = [
    gst-int-key dk-set-scheme "colour scheme" 0 0 (dk-scheme-count - 1),
    gst-int-key dk-set-adorn "adornments" dk-adorn-default 0 15,
    gst-int-key dk-set-sound "sounds" 0 0 1
  ]

  dk-settings-of : Integer -> SettingsStore
  dk-settings-of (ds) =
    let s0 = gst-store dk-settings-keys
    in let s1 = gst-or-keep s0 (gst-set s0 dk-set-scheme (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell))
    in let s2 = gst-or-keep s1 (gst-set s1 dk-set-adorn (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell))
    in gst-or-keep s2 (gst-set s2 dk-set-sound (peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell))

  dk-settings-save : Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  dk-settings-save (ds) = gst-save (dk-settings-of ds)

  dk-settings-into : Integer, SettingsStore -> Integer
  dk-settings-into (ds) (st) =
    let sp = poke-32 ds dk-scheme-cell (gst-get st dk-set-scheme 0)
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell (gst-get st dk-set-adorn dk-adorn-default)
    in poke-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell (gst-get st dk-set-sound 0)

Section: Sound

 The controller reads the descriptor list and the samples by DMA after the
 call that starts the stream has returned, so both have to outlive the
 playback rather than the call. `desk-loop` restores the heap on every poll,
 so the click is rendered once at boot into a block the base mark cannot
 reach, and the click itself only writes registers.

  dk-snd-blk-buf : Integer = 16
  dk-snd-blk-len : Integer = 20

  dk-snd-init : Integer -> Integer
  dk-snd-init (ds) =
    let fx = snd-click
    in let n = hda-sample-rate * fx.snd-duration / 1000
    in let blk = alloc-zeroed 32 128
    in let buf = hda-square-tone-at (fx.snd-freq) n (hda-amplitude * fx.snd-volume / 1000)
    in let b0 = poke-32 blk dk-snd-blk-buf buf
    in let b1 = poke-32 blk dk-snd-blk-len (n * 4)
    in poke-32 ds dk-sound-cell blk

  dk-snd-play : Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  dk-snd-play (ds) =
    let blk = peek-32 ds dk-sound-cell
    in if peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell == 0 then 0
    else if blk == 0 then 0
    else hda-play-pcm-at blk (peek-32 blk dk-snd-blk-buf) (peek-32 blk dk-snd-blk-len)

  dk-snd-click : Integer, Boolean -> [Audio] Integer
  dk-snd-click (ds) (clicked) = if clicked then dk-snd-play ds else 0

Section: Drawn Icons

 The chrome's icons are drawings rather than eight pixel masks, rasterized
 ONCE at boot at the size the gutter asks for. It has to be once and it has
 to be here: `desk-loop` restores the heap on every poll and a pane exit
 restores to the base mark, so artwork rendered during a paint would be freed
 while the cell still named it. This runs above the base mark, like every
 other pointer the desk keeps.

 The set and its order are `GopIcon`'s, not restated here: a name added there
 is rendered here without this chapter changing, which is the mistake the
 chrome's own icon list already made once by naming its six twice.

  dk-icons-size : Integer -> Integer
  dk-icons-size (blk) = peek-32 blk 0

  dk-icons-at : Integer, Text -> Integer
  dk-icons-at (blk) (name) = peek-32 blk (4 + 4 * gicon-drawn-slot name 0)

  dk-icons-render : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-icons-render (blk) (n) (i) =
    if i >= gicon-drawn-count then 0
    else let w = poke-32 blk (4 + 4 * i) (gicon-render (gicon-vec-named (gicon-drawn-name i)) n)
    in dk-icons-render blk n (i + 1)

  dk-icons-init : Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-icons-init (ds) (tf) (w) =
    let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let avail = desk-text-w tf ws desk-chrome-gutter
    in let scale = if avail < 8 then 1 else avail / 8
    in let n = 8 * scale
    in let blk = alloc-zeroed (8 + 4 * gicon-drawn-count) 64
    in let sp = poke-32 blk 0 n
    in let r = dk-icons-render blk n 0
    in poke-32 ds dk-icons-cell blk

Section: Chrome

  dk-corner-r : Integer = 6

  dk-corner : Integer -> CornerStyle
  dk-corner (f) = if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-round then CornerRound dk-corner-r else CornerSharp

  dk-bdr : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Border
  dk-bdr (f) (c) (n) = border-uniform (if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-border then n else 0) c (dk-corner f)

  dk-grad : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Gradient
  dk-grad (f) (a) (b) = if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-gradient then gradient-v a b else gradient-none

  dk-shade : Integer -> Shadow
  dk-shade (f) = if dk-adorn-on f dk-adorn-shadow then shadow-subtle 0 else shadow-none

  dk-theme-of : Integer, Integer -> Theme
  dk-theme-of (scheme) (f) =
    if dk-is-lang scheme then sk-theme (dk-lang scheme)
    else dk-theme-palette scheme f

  dk-theme-palette : Integer, Integer -> Theme
  dk-theme-palette (scheme) (f) =
    let pal = dk-scheme-palette scheme
    in let bdr = dk-bdr f (pal.pal-border) 1
    in let pad = edges-uniform 4
    in let mar = edges-uniform 2
    in let sh = dk-shade f
    in let base = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-fg) border-none pad edges-zero shadow-none (dk-grad f (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-muted)) accent-border-none
    in let btn = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-primary) (pal.pal-bg) bdr pad mar sh (dk-grad f (pal.pal-primary) (pal.pal-secondary)) accent-border-none
    in let btn-h = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-accent) (pal.pal-bg) bdr pad mar sh (dk-grad f (pal.pal-accent) (pal.pal-primary)) accent-border-none
    in let btn-p = widget-style-rich (pal.pal-secondary) (pal.pal-fg) bdr pad mar shadow-none (dk-grad f (pal.pal-secondary) (pal.pal-bg)) accent-border-none
    in let btn-d = widget-style (pal.pal-muted) (pal.pal-muted) bdr pad mar
    in let inp = widget-style (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-fg) (dk-bdr f (pal.pal-muted) 1) pad mar
    in let inp-f = widget-style (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-fg) (dk-bdr f (pal.pal-accent) 2) pad mar
    in let lbl = widget-style 0 (pal.pal-fg) border-none edges-zero mar
    in let sep = widget-style (pal.pal-border) (pal.pal-border) border-none edges-zero (edges-xy 0 4)
    in Theme {
      th-name = dk-scheme-name scheme,
      th-palette = pal,
      th-panel = state-styles-flat base,
      th-button = StateStyles { ss-normal = btn, ss-hover = btn-h, ss-pressed = btn-p, ss-disabled = btn-d, ss-focused = btn-h },
      th-label = state-styles-flat lbl,
      th-input = StateStyles { ss-normal = inp, ss-hover = inp, ss-pressed = inp, ss-disabled = btn-d, ss-focused = inp-f },
      th-gauge = state-styles-flat (widget-style (pal.pal-bg) (pal.pal-primary) bdr (edges-uniform 2) mar),
      th-separator = state-styles-flat sep
    }

  dk-theme-ds : Integer -> Theme
  dk-theme-ds (ds) = dk-theme-of (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell)

  dk-pal : Integer -> Palette
  dk-pal (ds) = dk-scheme-palette (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell)

  dk-wall : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wall (ds) = (dk-pal ds).pal-bg

  dk-gpu : Integer -> Integer
  dk-gpu (ds) = peek-32 ds desk-gpu-cell

Section: Window Metrics

 The welcome window is hand-drawn rather than laid out, so it is the one
 surface that has to do its own fitting. Its width in columns does not change
 with the scale, so its pixel height is the text height times the scale, and
 at scale 3 that is 1194 pixels against a 1080-pixel panel -- taller than the
 screen, with its title bar off the top. `dk-win-s` therefore picks the
 LARGEST scale that fits rather than trusting `ui-scale`.

 It is also centred in the CONTENT area rather than on the whole screen. On
 the screen it overlapped the sidebar at every width below about 800, and at
 scale 3 it overlapped at 1920 too, because the sidebar grew with the scale
 while a screen-centred window did not move.

  dk-sidebar-w : Integer = 160

  dk-win-fits : Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Boolean
  dk-win-fits (h) (tf) (s) = dk-win-h tf s * s + dk-task-h * s + dk-bar-h * s <= h

  dk-win-s : Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-win-s (w) (h) (tf) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in if dk-win-fits h tf s then s
    else if s > 1 & dk-win-fits h tf (s - 1) then s - 1
    else 1

  dk-win-x : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-win-x (w) (s) =
    let left = dk-sidebar-w * ui-scale w
    in left + (w - left - dk-win-w * s) / 2

  dk-win-y : Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-win-y (h) (s) (tf) = dk-bar-h * s + (h - dk-bar-h * s - dk-task-h * s - dk-win-h tf s * s) / 2

Section: RTC

 The CMOS RTC answers on ports 0x70/0x71 on every PC, virtual or
 real -- no VM convenience involved. Registers 0, 2, 4 are seconds,
 minutes, hours, BCD-encoded. `rtc-read` and the guarded `rtc-seconds`
 come from GopRtc, which waits out Update-In-Progress and reads twice:
 a raw seconds read from a fast poll loop lands in the update window
 constantly and answers garbage.

  dk-bcd : Integer -> Integer
  dk-bcd (n) = (n / 16) * 10 + int-mod n 16

  dk-pad2 : Integer -> Text
  dk-pad2 (n) = if n < 10 then "0" & show n else show n

Section: Chrome

 The shell chrome built with the real widget system: a sidebar column of
 app buttons pinned 160 wide, and a right-hand column of flexible content
 over a 24-pixel taskbar row. The compositor lays it out against the real
 panel rectangle and paints it in software.

 Port 0x403 IN is the codex-vm host rasterizer's present probe: it reads
 1 under codex-vm, and bus-float on a real machine with no GPU driver.

  desk-gpu-cell : Integer = 48

 Named, not a literal, and that is the whole point of it. The 64-byte block
 holds cells 0 to 60, and on 2026-08-11 two lanes independently took 48
 because nothing in the file said it was spoken for. A literal offset is
 invisible to the next person; a constant shows up in one grep.

  desk-console-cell : Integer = 36

 The Edit pane's state block. `desk-edit` read this cell from the day it was
 written and NOTHING ever wrote it, so `ged-run` was handed zero and
 `ged-init` parked the editor's 8 MB buffer and 1 MB line index at physical
 address 0. It worked because nothing else on this platform uses those bytes.
 Measured 2026-08-15 on the desk under codex-vm: `peek-32 0 0` reads 0 before
 the pane is opened and 0x639100 after, which is the buffer pointer.

  desk-edit-cell : Integer = 28

 The desk's base heap mark, taken in `desk-run` after the state blocks above
 are allocated and BEFORE the first `root`. A pane visit restores to it on the
 way out, which is what makes the visit free rather than 20 KB: the desk never
 unwinds -- every pane tail-calls `desk-loop` again -- so without a restore the
 root that visit rebuilds is stacked on top of the root it replaced, forever.
 Nothing allocated after this mark survives a pane exit, so a pointer parked in
 a `ds` cell must be allocated in `desk-run` above.

  desk-mark-cell : Integer = 4
  desk-prog-cell : Integer = 44
  desk-clock-cell : Integer = 60

  desk-gpu-present : Integer -> [Gpu.Compute] Integer
  desk-gpu-present (dummy) = if gpu-in #403 == 1 then 1 else 0

 The sidebar carries the launcher and a few pinned panes rather than one
 button per app. It held thirteen and the column was full at 1024x768, so
 the fourteenth app had nowhere to go; the groups in `GopPrograms` are
 where an app goes now, and adding one costs a row there instead of the
 only column here. The gpu branch went with it: both lists differed only
 by the 3D View button, and `gsc-new` takes `dk-gpu ds` and handles its
 absence itself, so the launcher offers that pane either way.

  desk-sidebar : Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-sidebar (gpu) =
    let items = [widget-label "brand" "CODEX", widget-button "programs" "   Programs", widget-button "files" "   Files", widget-button "edit" "   Edit", widget-button "console" "   Console", widget-set-flex (widget-label "sidebar-spacer" "") 1, widget-button "shutdown" "   Shutdown"]
    in widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "sidebar" DirColumn 1 items) 160 0) 0

  desk-task-clock-w : Integer = 220

 The `tasks` slot was an empty spacer until 2026-08-19. It reads the mark stack
 rather than a list of its own, which is what makes it correct by construction:
 an app is ALIVE exactly when it holds a live entry there, so the row cannot
 drift from the thing it reports. A dead entry takes `desk-focus-none` and names
 nothing. Only the five heavy panes ever push, so only they can appear.

 It is rebuilt by `desk-draw`, which runs at exactly the two moments the live set
 changes AND the desk is visible: `desk-app-hide` and `desk-app-close`. An open
 does not redraw the desk, and does not need to, because the pane covers it.

  desk-focus-name : Integer -> Text
  desk-focus-name (fid) =
    if fid == desk-focus-files then "Files"
    else if fid == desk-focus-bro then "Web"
    else if fid == desk-focus-scene then "3D"
    else if fid == desk-focus-fish then "Fish"
    else if fid == desk-focus-edit then "Edit"
    else ""

  desk-task-text : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  desk-task-text (ms) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= desk-marks-depth ms then acc
    else
      let nm = desk-focus-name (desk-marks-id ms i)
      in let acc2 = if text-length nm == 0 then acc else if text-length acc == 0 then nm else acc & "   " & nm
      in desk-task-text ms (i + 1) acc2

  desk-taskbar : Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-taskbar (ds) =
    let menu = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-button "menu" "   Codex") 72 0) 0
    in let tasks = widget-set-flex (widget-label "tasks" (desk-task-text (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) 0 "")) 1
    in let clock = widget-set-min (widget-label "task-clock" "") desk-task-clock-w 0
    in widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "taskbar" DirRow 4 [menu, tasks, clock]) 0 dk-task-h) 0

 BOTH columns clear the top bar, not just the right one. The sidebar used
 to be pushed clear by the panel style's own margin, and when that margin
 went so did the gap: at scale 1 the first sidebar button drew under the
 bar. The bar is painted by `desk-topbar` outside the widget tree, so
 nothing in the layout knows it is there unless it is told twice.

 The face reaches the CHROME only. A pane's own tree is handed through
 untouched because a pane that hit-tests its own subtree does so with plain
 `widget-layout` (`GopBrowser.codex`), and a cell fitted on one side of that
 pair and not the other puts a click on the wrong widget.

  desk-chrome-face : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> WidgetNode
  desk-chrome-face (content) (w) (ds) (tf) =
    let s = ui-wscale w
    in let gap = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "topgap" DirColumn 0 []) 0 (dk-bar-h * ui-scale w / ui-wscale w)) 0
    in let gap2 = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "topgap2" DirColumn 0 []) 0 (dk-bar-h * ui-scale w / ui-wscale w)) 0
    in let bar = comp-fit-text (desk-sidebar (dk-gpu ds)) tf s
    in let left = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "leftcol" DirColumn 0 [gap2, widget-set-flex bar 1]) 160 0) 0
    in widget-panel "root" DirRow 0 [left, widget-set-flex (widget-panel "right" DirColumn 0 [gap, widget-set-flex content 1, comp-fit-text (desk-taskbar ds) tf s]) 1]

  desk-chrome-with : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-chrome-with (content) (w) (ds) = desk-chrome-face content w ds gfont-none

 Every chrome render goes through here, and the reason is the taskbar clock.
 The band is painted from the widget tree, whose `task-clock` label is empty by
 design because the clock's TEXT is hand-painted over it once a second. So any
 chrome render ERASES the clock, and `desk-loop` runs `desk-clock` before the
 step, which means the pane wins the iteration and the band stays blank until
 the second turns over. Worse, the desk gates on `rtc-seconds` and the Clock
 pane on `rtc-seconds-unguarded`, so the two see the turnover on different
 iterations and which lands last was a race: measured, the clock was present at
 26 seconds open and absent at 14.

 Setting the gate cell to a value no second can equal makes the next
 `desk-clock` repaint unconditionally, and the next one is microseconds away
 rather than up to a second. The cost is one band repaint per chrome render
 instead of one per loop iteration, which is why it is done here and not by
 forcing the clock every time round.

 The chrome's own icons are the sidebar's five buttons and the taskbar's
 menu. They are named here rather than carried on the widgets because these
 six ids are the chrome and there is no list of them to add to: an app
 arrives in `gpr-entries`, not in the sidebar, and has since the sidebar
 stopped being one button per app.

 Each label carries two cells of leading gutter for the icon that lands at
 cell zero, the way a launcher row carries one after its selection mark.

  desk-chrome-gutter : Text = "   "

  desk-chrome-icons : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  desk-chrome-icons (base) (stride) (laid) (th) (ws) (tf) (ds) =
    let g = desk-chrome-gutter
    in let a = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "menu" "menu" ds
    in let b = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "programs" "grid" ds
    in let c = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "files" "folder" ds
    in let d = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "edit" "edit" ds
    in let f = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "console" "terminal" ds
    in desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf "" g "shutdown" "power" ds

  dk-chrome-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dk-chrome-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (tree) (ds) =
    let laid = comp-render base stride font tf (desk-chrome-face tree w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) w h (ui-wscale w)
    in let ic = desk-chrome-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) (ui-wscale w) tf ds
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in laid

Section: Drawing

  desk-line : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  desk-line (base) (stride) (h) (font) (s) (tf) (x) (y) (t) (fg) =
    if tf.gf-ok then gfont-text tf base stride h x y t fg
    else gop-draw-text base stride font x y t fg s 0

  dk-wrap-cols : Integer -> Integer
  dk-wrap-cols (s) =
    let c = (dk-win-w * s - 20 * s) / (8 * s)
    in if c < 8 then 8 else c

  dk-break : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-break (t) (limit) (i) (best) =
    if i >= limit then best
    else if is-whitespace (char-at t i) then dk-break t limit (i + 1) i
    else dk-break t limit (i + 1) best

 The panel is sized from the text rather than the text trimmed to the
 panel. The body used to be four hand-broken lines drawn WITHOUT the
 wrapper: 44, 45 and 47 characters against a 42-column width, so three of
 them ran 50 pixels past the right frame, and the wrapped hint below them
 fell 26 pixels past the bottom of a fixed 160. Both were measured off the
 glass. A hand-broken line cannot be checked by anything, so there are no
 hand-broken lines here now.

  dk-wrap-count : Text, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wrap-count (t) (cols) =
    let n = text-length t
    in if n <= cols then 1
    else let b = dk-break t cols 0 0
    in let cut = if b <= 0 then cols else b
    in let skip = if b <= 0 then cut else cut + 1
    in 1 + dk-wrap-count (substring t skip (n - skip)) cols

  dk-para-intro : Text = "The Codex desktop frame on the bare runtime. Input arrives over USB HID or PS/2; the clock runs on PIT ticks and the CMOS RTC. No emulator devices and no frame pacing."

  dk-para-hint : Text = "Programs (p) lists every pane by group, each with the letter that opens it from anywhere. The sidebar keeps only the pinned few: Files (f) browses this stick, Edit (e) opens a source file, Console (t) takes commands. Shutdown powers the machine off. Esc leaves a pane."

  dk-para-font : GopFont -> Text
  dk-para-font (tf) = if tf.gf-ok then "Font: TrueType CMUNSS.TTF from this stick's ESP." else "Font: CBF bitmap (no TTF on the ESP)."

  dk-body-lines : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-body-lines (tf) (s) =
    let cols = dk-wrap-cols s
    in dk-wrap-count dk-para-intro cols + dk-wrap-count (dk-para-font tf) cols + dk-wrap-count dk-para-hint cols

  dk-win-h : GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  dk-win-h (tf) (s) = dk-body-y0 + dk-body-lines tf s * dk-line-h + 2 * dk-para-gap + dk-win-pad-b

  dk-wrap-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-wrap-draw (base) (stride) (h) (font) (s) (tf) (x) (y) (t) (fg) (cols) =
    let n = text-length t
    in if n <= cols then desk-line base stride h font s tf x y t fg
    else let b = dk-break t cols 0 0
    in let cut = if b <= 0 then cols else b
    in let skip = if b <= 0 then cut else cut + 1
    in let l = desk-line base stride h font s tf x y (substring t 0 cut) fg
    in dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf x (y + 18 * s) (substring t skip (n - skip)) fg cols

  desk-topbar : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-topbar (base) (w) (stride) (font) (s) (ds) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let bar = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w (dk-bar-h * s) (pal.pal-border)
    in gop-draw-text base stride font (8 * s) (4 * s) "Codex" (pal.pal-accent) s 0

  desk-window : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  desk-window (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (s0) (tf) (ds) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let s = dk-win-s w h tf
    in let wx = dk-win-x w s
    in let wy = dk-win-y h s tf
    in let cols = dk-wrap-cols s
    in let tx = wx + 10 * s
    in let body = gop-fill-rect base stride wx wy (dk-win-w * s) (dk-win-h tf s * s) (pal.pal-muted)
    in let title = gop-fill-rect base stride wx wy (dk-win-w * s) (dk-title-h * s) (pal.pal-secondary)
    in let cap = gop-draw-text base stride font (wx + 6 * s) (wy + 2 * s) "Welcome" (pal.pal-accent) s 0
    in let y1 = wy + dk-body-y0 * s
    in let p1 = dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf tx y1 dk-para-intro (pal.pal-fg) cols
    in let y2 = y1 + (dk-wrap-count dk-para-intro cols * dk-line-h + dk-para-gap) * s
    in let p2 = dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf tx y2 (dk-para-font tf) (pal.pal-accent) cols
    in let y3 = y2 + (dk-wrap-count (dk-para-font tf) cols * dk-line-h + dk-para-gap) * s
    in dk-wrap-draw base stride h font s tf tx y3 dk-para-hint (pal.pal-primary) cols

  desk-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (s) (tf) (ds) =
    let wall = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in let laid = comp-render base stride font tf (desk-chrome-face (widget-panel "content" DirColumn 0 []) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) w h (ui-wscale w)
    in let ic = desk-chrome-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) (ui-wscale w) tf ds
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in let bar = desk-topbar base w stride font s ds
    in let win = desk-window base w h stride font s tf ds
    in laid

 The band carries a vertical gradient under the default adornments, so a flat
 fill sized to the text leaves a seam. `comp-walk` over the taskbar node costs
 one band-height fill and three labels once a second, against the full
 `comp-render` of the chrome that this loop must not pay for at that rate.

  desk-draw-clock : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Text, Integer, WidgetNode -> Integer
  desk-draw-clock (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (t) (ds) (root) =
    when widget-find root "taskbar"
      is None -> 0
      is Just (tb) -> desk-taskbar-clock base w h stride font tf t ds tb

 The clear is bounded by the FRAMEBUFFER and deliberately not by the band. A
 band-sized clip reads as the tighter and more obvious choice and it moved 408
 pixels on every pane, measured: the taskbar's own children carry styles whose
 boxes reach outside the node's bounds, so clipping to the band drops part of
 what the clear is there to repaint. The bound that belongs here is the one
 that cannot be wrong, which is the screen.

  desk-taskbar-clock : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Text, Integer, WidgetNode -> Integer
  desk-taskbar-clock (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (t) (ds) (tb) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let band = comp-mul-rect (tb.wn-bounds) ws
    in let clear = comp-walk base stride (layout-rect 0 0 w h) font tf tb (dk-theme-ds ds) ws
    in let ic = desk-icon-on base stride tb (dk-theme-ds ds) ws tf "" desk-chrome-gutter "menu" "menu" ds
    in let tw = text-length t * 8 * s
    in let want = band.lr-x + band.lr-w - tw - 8 * s
    in let tx = if want < band.lr-x then band.lr-x else want
    in let ty = band.lr-y + (band.lr-h - 14 * s) / 2
    in gop-draw-text base stride font tx ty t ((dk-pal ds).pal-accent) s 0

Section: Clock Gate

 It was the tick cell (28672, ~18 ticks per second) until 2026-08-04,
 when the ASUS TUF answered `pit=979` twice, 3405 seconds apart: the
 timer interrupt stops on that board at about 54 seconds and never
 resumes, while IRQ1 keeps delivering and the RTC keeps counting. A
 repaint gated on a dead counter never fires, so the clock froze and
 the Monitor pane below painted one frame for the life of the pane --
 which reads as a hung desktop and was reported as one. The RTC is
 read off ports 0x70/0x71 with no interrupt, no PIC and no handler in
 the path; a machine that boots has one.

  desk-second-cell : Integer = 8

  desk-clock : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) (root) = act
    now <- rtc-seconds 0
    if now < 0 then 0
    else if now == peek-32 ds desk-second-cell then 0
    else act
      hh <- rtc-read 4
      mm <- rtc-read 2
      let st = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell now
      in desk-draw-clock base w h stride font tf (dk-pad2 (dk-bcd hh) & ":" & dk-pad2 (dk-bcd mm) & ":" & dk-pad2 now) ds root
    end
  end

Section: Monitor

 What this machine is, read from the live sources at paint time rather
 than from anything cached at boot. It is the first pane worth having on
 a board nobody has booted before: the geometry row alone answers the
 question the whole 2026-08-02 display campaign was about, and it answers
 it on the glass without a serial port.

  dk-mon-x : Integer = 160
  dk-mon-row-h : Integer = 18

  dk-mon-val-dx : Integer = 100
  dk-mon-tick-cell : Integer = 28672

  dk-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dk-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  desk-mon-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, Integer, Text, Text, Integer -> Integer
  desk-mon-row (base) (stride) (h) (font) (s) (tf) (y) (k) (v) (ds) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let x = dk-mon-x + 12 * s
    in let lbl = desk-line base stride h font s tf x y k (pal.pal-primary)
    in desk-line base stride h font s tf (x + dk-mon-val-dx * s) y v (pal.pal-fg)

  desk-mon-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, AcpiInfo, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-mon-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (s) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ai) (clk) (ticks) (ds) =
    let pal = dk-pal ds
    in let top = dk-bar-h * s
    in let ph = h - dk-task-h * s - top
    in let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride dk-mon-x top (w - dk-mon-x) ph (pal.pal-bg)
    in let y0 = top + 12 * s
    in let hd = desk-line base stride h font s tf (dk-mon-x + 12 * s) y0 "SYSTEM MONITOR" (pal.pal-accent)
    in let r = dk-mon-row-h * s
    in let y1 = y0 + 2 * r
    in let a = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf y1 "display" (show w & " x " & show h & "   stride " & show stride) ds
    in let b = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + r) "framebuffer" ("0x" & hex-i64 base & "   scale " & show s) ds
    in let c = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 2 * r) "input" ("keyboard " & (if kbd.uk-ok then "USB HID" else "mailbox") & "   mouse " & dk-yn (mouse.um-ok)) ds
    in let d = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 3 * r) "font" (if tf.gf-ok then "TrueType CMUNSS.TTF   cell " & show (tf.gf-gh) & "   baseline " & show (tf.gf-asc) & "   cap " & show (tf.gf-cap) else "CBF bitmap") ds
    in let e = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 4 * r) "acpi" (if ai.ai-ok then "rev " & show (ai.ai-revision) & "   tables " & show (ai.ai-tables) else "no RSDP: stub, EBDA and BIOS area all empty") ds
    in let f = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 5 * r) "lapic" ("0x" & hex-i32 (ai.ai-lapic) & "   ioapic 0x" & hex-i32 (ai.ai-ioapic)) ds
    in let g = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 6 * r) "power" ("S5 " & dk-yn (ai.ai-s5-ok) & "   reset " & dk-yn (ai.ai-reset-ok) & "   sci " & show (ai.ai-sci)) ds
    in let i = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 7 * r) "clock" (clk & "   ticks " & show ticks) ds
    in let j = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 8 * r) "memory" ("heap frontier 0x" & hex-i64 __heap-save & "   desk mark 0x" & hex-i64 (peek-32 ds desk-mark-cell)) ds
    in let k = desk-mon-row base stride h font s tf (y1 + 9 * r) "stack" desk-stack-text ds
    in desk-line base stride h font s tf (dk-mon-x + 12 * s) (top + ph - 20 * s) "Esc returns." (pal.pal-primary)

 The stack half of the arena had no instrument, which is the half of stage 0
 that stayed open longest. It has one now, and it is a READ of two cells the
 compiler already maintains rather than anything new.

 `28736` and `4072` are `stack-min-rsp-addr` and `ram-size-addr` from
 `codex/compiler/Emit/X86_64Boot.codex`. They are literals here because this
 chapter cannot cite the compiler, so they are hand-synced and will go stale
 silently if that file moves them.

 **The reading is honest in an ordinary build rather than absent, and that is
 the point.** The chapter's boot prologue stores `ram-size` into the minimum
 cell UNCONDITIONALLY (`X86_64Chapter.codex:385`), and only the per-function
 prologue narrows it, guarded by `if st1.trace-alloc` (`X86_64.codex:12`). So
 the two cells read EQUAL whenever nothing ever wrote a lower RSP, and the row
 says so rather than printing a depth of zero as though it had measured one.

 **It answers now, in a `-Trace` build, since root threaded the mode word
 through `compile-to-cdx-with-exit-mode` at main 17934.** Measured 2026-08-20
 on this pane: an ordinary desk still reads never-narrowed, which is correct
 and not a fault, while a `compile.ps1 -Trace` desk reads
 `min rsp 0x00000000bdffd700   depth 10496 B`. The two builds are also no
 longer the same size, 1,156,287 bytes against 1,085,879, which is the
 per-function prologue being emitted at last.

 That the sizes were IDENTICAL is what this block used to record, on
 2026-08-19, as the evidence that no CDX the shipped path produced could
 narrow the cell: `opening.codex` passed the emitter's `trace` argument as a
 literal `False` and took no parameter that could carry the mode word. The
 diagnosis was right and the fix was the one prescribed; only the tense here
 was left behind, which is what a measured claim does when somebody else
 repairs the thing it measured.

  desk-stack-min-addr : Integer = 28736
  desk-stack-top-addr : Integer = 4072

  desk-stack-text : Text
  desk-stack-text =
    let mn = peek-qword desk-stack-min-addr 0
    in let tp = peek-qword desk-stack-top-addr 0
    in if mn >= tp then "never narrowed -- this CDX was not built with -Trace"
    else "min rsp 0x" & hex-i64 mn & "   depth " & show (tp - mn) & " B"

 Repainted on the same RTC-second gate the taskbar clock uses, so the
 time is live rather than frozen at entry. The `ticks` figure it prints
 is the PIT cell READ rather than waited on: on a board whose timer
 dies it stops climbing while the rest of the pane keeps updating,
 which turns a fault that used to freeze this pane into a number
 reporting itself. The bracket is what keeps an open
 pane from growing: every row builds Text that nothing outside the frame
 reads. `ai` is parsed once in desk-monitor, below the mark, because
 re-walking the ACPI tables every second would be work with no answer
 that can change.

 The monitor repaints once a second on the RTC edge, so the cursor is hidden
 across that repaint and put back after it. Without the hide, the second
 that redraws under a displayed arrow leaves the save buffer describing
 pixels that no longer exist, and the next pointer move stamps them back.

 Monitor is a STEP, not a loop. It handles ONE event and returns: 1 to say it
 is still open, 0 to say it is done. `desk-loop` keeps the keyboard, so the
 desk goes on polling, painting its clock and answering the mouse while this
 pane is up, which is the whole point of the model and the thing the arm
 measures.

 The bracket stays where `desk-mon-loop` had it, around one event rather than
 around one iteration, because that is the same allocation either way: every
 row builds Text that nothing outside reads. It matters more here, not less --
 `desk-loop` has no bracket of its own, so an unbracketed step would leak into
 a loop that never unwinds.

 `acpi-boot` moved INSIDE the step and now runs on the repaint edge instead of
 once at pane entry. Its old home was a `let` above the loop, which a step has
 nowhere to put: state that outlives a step has to live in `ds` or in
 `desk-run`, and `AcpiInfo` is a record, so parking it in a cell would need a
 cast that the state blocks in this file deliberately avoid. A table walk once
 a second inside the bracket is the cheaper of the two, and the prose that used
 to justify hoisting it was arguing against re-walking every FRAME, which is
 not what this does.

 A step keeps INTEGERS in `ds` cells and RAW ADDRESSES in the state blocks
 `desk-run` allocates. Neither can hold a typed value, and the five panes still
 running their own loops all carry one: Files a `Fat16Volume` and a directory
 listing and the stack of where it has been, Browser a `BrowserState`, Edit a
 volume and a filename, the two 3D panes a render target and a view. This
 record is the channel for those, threaded beside `root`, and it is why
 `desk-loop` carries it rather than a cell doing so.

 **Where a value in here LIVES, which is the question that decides whether it
 works.** A pane's `-open` runs after `desk-run` took the base mark and before
 `desk-loop` takes its per-iteration mark, so anything it allocates sits ABOVE
 the base mark and BELOW the frame mark: the per-iteration restore never
 reaches it and it survives every step for as long as the pane is open. That is
 exactly the lifetime a step needs and it needs no new machinery.

 **What it does NOT survive is a close**, and that is deliberate rather than a
 gap. `desk-app-close` restores to the base mark, which reclaims the pane's
 entry paint and its state together, because the app is going away. State that
 must outlive a close still belongs in a `ds` block allocated in `desk-run`,
 which is how the Calculator remembers its number and the Console its
 scrollback across visits.

 **And the limit worth naming before someone meets it.** Several apps alive at
 once, rather than one focused app with its state, would need the base mark to
 rise when an app opens and fall when it quits. The heap is a bump allocator
 with save and restore and nothing else, so that is a mark STACK, and a mark
 stack is only correct if apps quit in the order they opened. That is a
 separate step and this record does not pretend to it.

 **`GopEdit`'s 9 MB does not move in here and must not.** It is allocated
 lazily and its POINTER is parked in a `ds` cell; only Edit's TYPED values,
 its volume and filename, belong here.

 **This block said the cell has no restore, and that was wrong.** The pointer
 survives a restore because the cell is below the base mark; the MEMORY it
 names does not, because `ged-init` runs from `desk-edit-open`, which is above
 it. Measured 2026-08-19 and corrected in the Editor section below, where the
 close-path fix lives.

  FilesState = record {
    fs-vol : Fat16Volume,
    fs-entries : List Fat16DirEntry,
    fs-clusters : List Integer,
    fs-names : List Text,
    fs-sel : Integer,
    fs-top : Integer,
    fs-ptr : Integer,
    fs-mode : Integer
  }

  DeskApps = record {
    da-files : Maybe FilesState,
    da-browser : Maybe BrowserPane,
    da-scene : Maybe ScenePane,
    da-edit : Maybe EditState
  }

  desk-apps-empty : DeskApps
  desk-apps-empty = DeskApps { da-files = None, da-browser = None, da-scene = None, da-edit = None }

 Cell 20, free until val took it 2026-08-19; announced in the contract. The
 block is 128 bytes allocated in `desk-run` before the base mark: offset 0 is
 the depth, entry `i` is a focus id at `8 * i + 8` and its mark at
 `8 * i + 12`. A dead entry keeps its mark and takes id 0, which is
 `desk-focus-none` and can never name a live app.

  desk-marks-cell : Integer = 20
  desk-marks-max : Integer = 8

  desk-marks-depth : Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-depth (ms) = peek-32 ms 0

  desk-marks-id : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-id (ms) (i) = peek-32 ms (8 * i + 8)

  desk-marks-mark : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-mark (ms) (i) = peek-32 ms (8 * i + 12)

  desk-marks-push : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-push (ms) (fid) (mark) =
    let d = desk-marks-depth ms
    in if d >= desk-marks-max then d
    else
      let a = poke-32 ms (8 * d + 8) fid
      in let b = poke-32 ms (8 * d + 12) mark
      in poke-32 ms 0 (d + 1)

  desk-marks-clear : Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-clear (ms) = poke-32 ms 0 0

  desk-marks-find : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-find (ms) (fid) (i) =
    if i < 0 then 0 - 1
    else if desk-marks-id ms i == fid then i
    else desk-marks-find ms fid (i - 1)

  desk-marks-kill : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-kill (ms) (fid) =
    let i = desk-marks-find ms fid (desk-marks-depth ms - 1)
    in if i < 0 then 0 else poke-32 ms (8 * i + 8) desk-focus-none

  desk-marks-reclaim : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-marks-reclaim (ms) (acc) =
    let d = desk-marks-depth ms
    in if d <= 0 then acc
    else if desk-marks-id ms (d - 1) /= desk-focus-none then acc
    else
      let m = desk-marks-mark ms (d - 1)
      in let p = poke-32 ms 0 (d - 1)
      in desk-marks-reclaim ms m

  desk-marks-any-live : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  desk-marks-any-live (ms) (i) =
    if i < 0 then False
    else if desk-marks-id ms i /= desk-focus-none then True
    else desk-marks-any-live ms (i - 1)

  desk-focus-cell : Integer = 12
  desk-focus-none : Integer = 0
  desk-focus-mon : Integer = 1
  desk-focus-cal : Integer = 2
  desk-focus-style : Integer = 3
  desk-focus-calc : Integer = 4
  desk-focus-clkp : Integer = 5
  desk-focus-prog : Integer = 6
  desk-focus-dif : Integer = 7
  desk-focus-trk : Integer = 8
  desk-focus-con : Integer = 9
  desk-focus-files : Integer = 10
  desk-focus-bro : Integer = 11
  desk-focus-scene : Integer = 12
  desk-focus-fish : Integer = 13
  desk-focus-edit : Integer = 14
  desk-focus-menu : Integer = 15
  desk-focus-rev : Integer = 16

  desk-review-cell : Integer = 0

  desk-mon-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-mon-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      now <- rtc-seconds 0
      if sc == 1 then let d = __heap-restore mark in 0
      else if sc == 88 then act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
      else if now < 0 | now == peek-32 ds 16 then
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      else act
        hh <- rtc-read 4
        mm <- rtc-read 2
        let st = poke-32 ds 16 now
        in let ai = acpi-boot 0
        in let clk = dk-pad2 (dk-bcd hh) & ":" & dk-pad2 (dk-bcd mm) & ":" & dk-pad2 now
        in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
        in let p = desk-mon-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf kbd mouse ai clk (peek-32 dk-mon-tick-cell 0) ds
        in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in let dropped = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
  end

 One dispatcher, so `desk-loop` names no pane. A pane joins by taking a focus
 id, adding a line here and a line in `desk-dispatch`, which is the same
 two-places rule section 4 of the contract already states for a loop pane.

  desk-step-of : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-step-of (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (foc) (sc) (clicked) =
    if foc == desk-focus-mon then desk-mon-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-cal then desk-quiet-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-dif then desk-quiet-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-trk then desk-trk-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-rev then desk-rev-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-con then desk-console-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-style then desk-style-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-calc then desk-calc-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-clkp then desk-clock-pane-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-prog then desk-prog-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-menu then desk-menu-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-files then desk-files-step base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-bro then desk-browser-step base w h stride font tf mouse ds cs apps root sc clicked
    else if foc == desk-focus-scene then desk-scene-step base w h stride font kbd mouse cs apps gsc-scene sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-fish then desk-scene-step base w h stride font kbd mouse cs apps gf-scene sc
    else if foc == desk-focus-edit then desk-edit-step base w h stride font mouse ds cs apps sc clicked
    else 0

 A still picture is a whole class of pane, not one pane. The Calendar draws its
 month once and the Diffusion pane draws its form once; neither repaints, and
 for both the entry paint happens where focus is taken, so every later call
 only has to answer Esc and F12. One step serves both, and the next such pane
 costs a line in `desk-step-of` and nothing else.

 The mouse is not their business either: `desk-loop` pumps it and moves the
 cursor before any step is called, which is what `dk-pump` used to do inside
 each pane's own loop.

  desk-quiet-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-quiet-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) =
    if sc == 1 then 0
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else 1

  desk-app-close-to : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
 Both close paths DROP the `apps` they were handed and build an empty one after
 the restore, and that is not tidiness. The restore frees everything above the
 base mark, which is exactly where a pane's `-open` put its state, so carrying
 the old record forward would carry pointers into memory that has just been
 handed back. The fresh record is built AFTER the restore, so it lives beside
 the new `root` and dies with it.

  desk-app-close-to (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (sc) =
    let fid = peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell
    in let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let sb = apps.da-browser
    in let ss = apps.da-scene
    in let se = apps.da-edit
    in let sfi = apps.da-files
    in let kl = desk-marks-kill ms fid
    in let rec = desk-marks-reclaim ms (0 - 1)
    in let dep = desk-marks-depth ms
    in let cf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-none
    in let hd2 = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let tgt = if dep <= 0 then peek-32 ds desk-mark-cell else if rec < 0 then __heap-save else rec
    in let eb = peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell
    in let ev = if peek-32 eb 0 /= 0 & tgt <= peek-32 eb 0 then poke-32 eb 0 0 else 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore tgt
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let nf = if fid == desk-focus-files then None else sfi
    in let nb = if fid == desk-focus-bro then None else sb
    in let nsc = if fid == desk-focus-scene | fid == desk-focus-fish then None else ss
    in let ne = if fid == desk-focus-edit then None else se
    in let fresh = if dep <= 0 then desk-apps-empty else DeskApps { da-files = nf, da-browser = nb, da-scene = nsc, da-edit = ne }
    in let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-dispatch base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs fresh root sc False

  desk-step-hide : Integer = 1000000

  desk-app-hide : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-app-hide (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) =
    let cf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-none
    in let hd2 = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root

  desk-app-close : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-app-close (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) =
    let fid = peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell
    in let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let sb = apps.da-browser
    in let ss = apps.da-scene
    in let se = apps.da-edit
    in let sfi = apps.da-files
    in let kl = desk-marks-kill ms fid
    in let rec = desk-marks-reclaim ms (0 - 1)
    in let dep = desk-marks-depth ms
    in let cf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-none
    in let hd2 = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let tgt = if dep <= 0 then peek-32 ds desk-mark-cell else if rec < 0 then __heap-save else rec
    in let eb = peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell
    in let ev = if peek-32 eb 0 /= 0 & tgt <= peek-32 eb 0 then poke-32 eb 0 0 else 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore tgt
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let nf = if fid == desk-focus-files then None else sfi
    in let nb = if fid == desk-focus-bro then None else sb
    in let nsc = if fid == desk-focus-scene | fid == desk-focus-fish then None else ss
    in let ne = if fid == desk-focus-edit then None else se
    in let fresh = if dep <= 0 then desk-apps-empty else DeskApps { da-files = nf, da-browser = nb, da-scene = nsc, da-edit = ne }
    in let c0 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs fresh root
    end
  end

Section: Calculator

  desk-calc-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-calc-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) =
    let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (gcalc-tree ks) ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

  desk-calc-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-calc-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-calc-draw base w h stride font tf ks ds

 The keypad is clickable. `dk-calc-hit` lays the same tree out again and
 answers the scancode of whatever button is under the pointer, so a click
 and a keystroke reach `gcalc-key` by the same road.

  dk-calc-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-calc-hit (w) (h) (ks) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (gcalc-tree ks) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) -> gcalc-id-scan (wgt.wn-id)
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

  desk-calc-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-calc-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ks) (ds) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = desk-calc-draw base w h stride font tf ks ds
    in dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse

 `gcalc-key` APPLIES the key and answers whether it meant anything, so the
 test in the fourth arm is the press: it must be evaluated exactly once, and
 the arm below it only repaints. Calling it again to find out what it did
 would enter the digit twice. That was true of the loop this replaces and is
 the one thing about this pane a conversion could quietly break.

  desk-calc-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-calc-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) =
    let ks = peek-32 ds 24
    in let hit = if clicked then dk-calc-hit w h ks (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
    in if sc == 1 then 0
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if hit /= 0 then
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let a = gcalc-key ks hit
      in let p = desk-calc-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
      in let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 1
    else if gcalc-key ks sc == 0 then 1
    else
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let p = desk-calc-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
      in let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 1

Section: The Console Pane

 The pane owns its input line and scrollback and calls GopConsole for every
 key, which is the same division desk-calc has with GopCalc: the desk owns
 the loop and the chrome, the chapter owns the state and what a key means.

 The wall fill is the ENTRY paint only. It writes every pixel on the
 screen, and doing it again before each redraw is what made typing flash:
 the eye catches the cleared frame because there is no back buffer and
 nothing pages the two apart. The widget layer draws its own opaque
 panels over the same rectangles every time, so the wall underneath them
 has nothing left to contribute after the first frame -- the clear was
 pure cost and pure flicker.

  desk-console-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-console-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ns) (ds) =
    let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (gcon-tree ns) ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

  desk-console-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-console-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ns) (ds) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-console-draw base w h stride font tf ns ds

  desk-console-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-console-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ns) (ds) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = desk-console-draw base w h stride font tf ns ds
    in dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse

 `mods`, the shift state, was a loop parameter and goes to the console's own
 state block for the same reason the Clock's `last` did: `ds` has two cells
 left and this is not worth one. **`GopConsole` owns offsets 0 to 12 of that
 block and the desk owns 16 up**, the same split as the clock block, written
 here because nothing cross-checks it.

  dk-conp-mods-cell : Integer = 16

  desk-console-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-console-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) =
    let ns = peek-32 ds desk-console-cell
    in let md = gcon-shift-of (peek-32 ns dk-conp-mods-cell) sc
    in let sm = poke-32 ns dk-conp-mods-cell md
    in if sc == 1 then 0
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 0 then 1
    else act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        a <- gcon-key ns sc md
        let p = desk-console-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ns ds
        in let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end

  desk-console-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-console-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ns) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let e = gcon-ensure ns
    in let p0 = desk-console-paint base w h stride font tf ns ds
    in let m0 = poke-32 ns dk-conp-mods-cell 0
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-con
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Clock

 The face is rebuilt from the part on every pass rather than cached, so
 the pane cannot drift away from the hardware it is showing. That means
 this loop repaints on a tick like the Monitor pane does, and unlike the
 panes that only repaint on a key.

  desk-clock-read : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock-read (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) = act
    idle <- rtc-wait-idle rtc-uip-fuel
    b <- clk-mode 0
    hh <- clk-hour-now b
    mi <- clk-field-value clk-reg-minutes b
    ss <- clk-field-value clk-reg-seconds b
    dd <- clk-field-value clk-reg-day b
    mo <- clk-field-value clk-reg-month b
    yy <- clk-field-value clk-reg-year b
    let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (clk-tree ks hh mi ss dd mo yy) ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds
  end

  desk-clock-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ks) (ds) = act
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-clock-read base w h stride font tf ks ds
  end

  desk-clock-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ks) (ds) = act
    hd <- act cursor-hide cs base stride end
    p <- desk-clock-read base w h stride font tf ks ds
    dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
  end

 The tick gate is the RTC second rather than a spin count, for the reason
 `desk-clock` records: the PIT stops on the ASUS at about 54 seconds and
 a repaint gated on a dead counter never fires again.

 `last`, the second this pane painted, was a loop PARAMETER and a step has no
 loop to carry one in. It goes in the pane's own state block rather than a `ds`
 cell: `ds` has two cells left and this is a repaint cache, while the clock
 block is 64 bytes of which `GopClock` uses 0 to 36. **Offsets 40 and up in
 that block are the desk's, 0 to 36 are `GopClock`'s**, and that split is
 written here and in the contract because nothing in the tree cross-checks it.

  dk-clkp-last-cell : Integer = 40

 The F12 arm is NEW. The contract requires every pane to carry it and this
 loop never did, so the screenshot key was silently dead in the clock pane;
 `clk-key` answers 0 for scancode 88 and the key fell through to the ignore
 arm. Converting the pane is when that shows up, so it is fixed here.

  desk-clock-pane-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-clock-pane-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      now <- rtc-seconds-unguarded 0
      let ks = peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
      in if sc == 88 then act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
      else if sc == 0 then
        if now == peek-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell then
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        else act
          p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
          let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
      else act
        let a = clk-key ks sc
        in if a == 3 then let d = __heap-restore mark in 0
        else if a == 2 then act
          l <- clk-load ks
          p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
          let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
        else if a == 4 then act
          c <- clk-commit ks
          z <- zone-save (clk-zone ks)
          let e = poke-32 ks clk-editing-cell 0
          in act
            p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
            let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
            in let d = __heap-restore mark
            in 1
          end
        end
        else if a == 1 then act
          p <- desk-clock-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ks ds
          let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell now
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
        else
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
      end
    end
  end

  desk-clock-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-clock-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ks) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    e <- clk-ensure ks
    act
      p0 <- desk-clock-paint base w h stride font tf ks ds
      c1 <- act dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse end
      let u = poke-32 ks dk-clkp-last-cell (0 - 1)
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-clkp
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Programs

 The launcher pane. It answers a SCANCODE and hands it to `desk-dispatch`,
 which is the same road a keystroke takes, so adding an app to
 `gpr-entries` needs no change here and no second dispatch table.

 Icons ride the LAID tree and not the widget tree, which is the same road
 `desk-taskbar-clock` takes over the band it repaints. A node carrying an
 icon child would be found by `ev-hit-widget` before its parent, so every
 click on an icon would resolve to a node the dispatch table has never
 heard of; painting after the walk cannot move a hit test at all.

 The bounds on a laid tree are LOGICAL, so a device rectangle is
 `comp-mul-rect` of the content box, exactly as the compositor does it. The
 icon centres on the content box rather than on the glyph height, because
 the glyph height depends on which face loaded and the box does not.

 An icon sits in the LEADING GUTTER of a label, so where it starts and how
 much room it has are both questions about rendered TEXT, and neither can be
 answered by counting characters. A loaded face is proportional: a string's
 width is not its length times anything, and three spaces of gutter are far
 narrower than three 8 pixel cells. This measured the gutter as
 `cells * comp-cell-w * ws` until 2026-08-20, which is the monospace
 assumption `GopFont`'s own prose warns about, and the moment the labels
 were centred properly and the face was actually rendering, every icon on
 the launcher and the sidebar sat on top of its first letter.

 So the caller names the two STRINGS instead: what precedes the icon, and
 the gutter the icon occupies. Both are measured through the face when there
 is one, and fall back to the cell only when there is not.

 The icon is then scaled to the room it actually has rather than to a fixed
 multiple, which is what keeps it inside the gutter at any face and any
 scale. Eight is the artwork's width; a gutter narrower than eight device
 pixels still draws, at scale 1, rather than vanishing.

  desk-text-w : GopFont, Integer, Text -> Integer
  desk-text-w (tf) (ws) (t) =
    if tf.gf-ok then gfont-text-w tf t else text-length t * comp-cell-w * ws

 A drawing is used when one exists for the name AND it was rasterized at the
 size this gutter asks for. The size is settled in `desk-run` from the same
 expression below, so the two agree on every ordinary paint; a face or a
 scale that moved since boot makes them disagree, and the eight pixel mask
 draws instead of a wrong size being stretched.

  desk-icon-in : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, Text, Text, Text, Integer -> Integer
  desk-icon-in (base) (stride) (n) (th) (ws) (tf) (prefix) (gutter) (name) (ds) =
    let st = widget-resolve-style n th
    in let c = comp-mul-rect (box-content-from-style (n.wn-bounds) st) ws
    in let avail = desk-text-w tf ws gutter
    in let scale = if avail < 8 then 1 else avail / 8
    in let ih = 8 * scale
    in let iy = c.lr-y + (if c.lr-h > ih then (c.lr-h - ih) / 2 else 0)
    in let ix = c.lr-x + desk-text-w tf ws prefix
    in let blk = peek-32 ds dk-icons-cell
    in if blk == 0 then gicon-blit base stride (gicon-named name) ix iy scale (st.ws-fg)
    else if gicon-drawn name == False then gicon-blit base stride (gicon-named name) ix iy scale (st.ws-fg)
    else if dk-icons-size blk /= ih then gicon-blit base stride (gicon-named name) ix iy scale (st.ws-fg)
    else gicon-blit-cov base stride (dk-icons-at blk name) ih ix iy (st.ws-fg)

  desk-icon-on : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, Text, Text, Text, Text, Integer -> Integer
  desk-icon-on (base) (stride) (laid) (th) (ws) (tf) (prefix) (gutter) (id) (name) (ds) =
    when widget-find laid id
      is Just (n) -> desk-icon-in base stride n th ws tf prefix gutter name ds
      is None -> 0

 `gpr-entries` is a module-level list, so every `gpr-at` rebuilds all
 fourteen records. The list is bound ONCE here and indexed, which is why
 this loop carries it and a length rather than calling `gpr-at`.

 The prefix is the row's own selection mark and not a constant, because
 `> ` and two spaces are different WIDTHS in a proportional face. The label
 shifts by that difference on the selected row, so the icon has to shift
 with it or it sits under the `C` of one row in fourteen.

  desk-gpr-icons : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, GopFont, List GprEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-gpr-icons (base) (stride) (laid) (th) (ws) (tf) (es) (n) (sel) (i) (ds) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let e = list-at es i
    in let d = desk-icon-on base stride laid th ws tf (gpr-mark i sel) (gpr-gutter) ("gpr-" & e.ge-label) (e.ge-icon) ds
    in desk-gpr-icons base stride laid th ws tf es n sel (i + 1) ds

  desk-programs-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-programs-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ps) (ds) =
    let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (gpr-tree ps) ds
    in let es = gpr-entries
    in let ic = desk-gpr-icons base stride laid (dk-theme-ds ds) (ui-wscale w) tf es (list-length es) (gpr-sel ps) 0 ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

  desk-programs-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-programs-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ps) (ds) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-programs-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds

  desk-programs-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-programs-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ps) (ds) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = desk-programs-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds
    in dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse

  dk-prog-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-prog-hit (w) (h) (ps) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (gpr-tree ps) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) -> gpr-id-scan (wgt.wn-id) 0
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

 The launcher is the pane that made the step protocol grow a third answer. It
 does not merely close: it closes AND names the app to open, which is the whole
 point of it. **A step returns 0 to close, 1 to stay open, and anything else as
 a SCANCODE to dispatch after closing.** That is unambiguous rather than a
 convention: every scancode `desk-dispatch` tests is 2 or more, 1 is Esc and
 already means close, and 0 is no key. So the launcher's answer travels as
 itself and no cell has to carry it.

 `gpr-key` moves the selection as well as answering, so like `gcalc-key` it is
 evaluated exactly once.

  desk-prog-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-prog-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) =
    let ps = peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    in let hit = if clicked then dk-prog-hit w h ps (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
    in if sc == 1 then 0
    else if hit /= 0 then hit
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 0 then 1
    else
      let chosen = gpr-key ps sc
      in if chosen /= 0 then chosen
      else
        let mark = __heap-save
        in let p = desk-programs-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ps ds
        in let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1

 The system menu is the LAUNCHER, placed differently. `gpr-tree` is the whole
 of its content and `gpr-key` and `gpr-id-scan` are the whole of its behaviour,
 so a row and the key it names still arrive by the same road and there is no
 second table to keep in step. What this adds is placement: two flex spacers
 pin the launcher's own tree to the bottom-left of the content area, above the
 taskbar button that opens it, instead of filling the region.

 It shares `desk-prog-cell` rather than taking a cell of its own, and the
 shared SELECTION is the point rather than a saving: the two presentations are
 one model, so a row chosen in one is the row chosen in the other.

 `widget-set-min` preserves flex, which is the trap `BROWSER-2` records, so
 every wrapper here sets flex explicitly. A menu column that stayed flexible
 would stretch to the full height and stop being a menu.

 **One column, where the launcher uses two, and that is a placement decision
 rather than a different model.** `gpr-tree` splits into two flex-1 columns
 because it is given the whole content region to fill; dropped into a
 bottom-anchored box it lays out wrong in a way worth recording, because the
 mechanism is `BROWSER-2`'s. The inner `widget-set-flex cols 1` has no height
 to divide when the enclosing box is sized to its own content, so `cols`
 collapses and the help line is drawn over the group headings. Measured on the
 glass, not feared. Building the rows directly avoids the question and gives a
 natural width off the widest label instead of half the desk.

  desk-menu-w : Integer = 500

 **It uses `gpr-tree` whole, two columns and all, and that is not laziness.**
 A single column of thirteen entries and four headings needs 366 logical plus
 padding against a content region of 400 at 1600x900, and nothing in the widget
 layer clips or scrolls, so the tail draws over the taskbar and off the glass.
 Measured three times while trying to avoid this: at the launcher's row height
 two entries fall off, and shrinking rows to 18 to make them fit clips the
 button chrome against its own label. The launcher split into two columns for
 exactly this reason and says so in its own prose. So the menu takes that tree
 unchanged and changes only the BOX: bounded width, left-anchored, opened from
 the taskbar. `gpr-tree` lays out correctly here because it is given a
 full-height box, which is the one thing its internal `widget-set-flex cols 1`
 needs.

  desk-menu-tree : Integer -> WidgetNode
  desk-menu-tree (ps) =
    let panel = widget-set-flex (gpr-tree ps) 1
    in let col = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel "sysmenu-col" DirColumn 0 [panel]) desk-menu-w 0) 0
    in let rgap = widget-set-flex (widget-panel "sysmenu-rgap" DirColumn 0 []) 1
    in widget-panel "sysmenu-root" DirRow 0 [col, rgap]

  desk-menu-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-menu-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ps) (ds) =
    let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (desk-menu-tree ps) ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

  desk-menu-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-menu-repaint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (cs) (mouse) (ps) (ds) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = desk-menu-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds
    in dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse

  dk-menu-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-menu-hit (w) (h) (ps) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (desk-menu-tree ps) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) -> gpr-id-scan (wgt.wn-id) 0
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

  desk-menu-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-menu-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) =
    let ps = peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    in let hit = if clicked then dk-menu-hit w h ps (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
    in if sc == 1 then 0
    else if hit /= 0 then hit
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 0 then 1
    else
      let chosen = gpr-key ps sc
      in if chosen /= 0 then chosen
      else
        let mark = __heap-save
        in let p = desk-menu-repaint base w h stride font tf cs mouse ps ds
        in let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1

  desk-menu-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-menu-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ps) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let e = gpr-ensure ps
    in let p0 = desk-menu-draw base w h stride font tf ps ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-menu
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

  desk-programs-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-programs-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ps) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let e = gpr-ensure ps
    in let p0 = desk-programs-paint base w h stride font tf ps ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-prog
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

  desk-calc-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-calc-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (ks) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let p0 = desk-calc-paint base w h stride font tf ks ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-calc
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Calendar

 The calendar pane. The month is a still picture until the date changes,
 so it is painted once on entry and the loop then only waits for Esc.
 There is no tick gate and no repaint: a calendar that repainted every
 second would be writing the same pixels 86,399 times a day to be right
 once.

 RTC registers 7, 8 and 9 are day of month, month and year, BCD like the
 time registers. The year register carries two digits, so the century is
 the caller's to supply and this assumes 2000.

  desk-cal-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-cal-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) = act
    yy <- rtc-read 9
    mm <- rtc-read 8
    dd <- rtc-read 7
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in let tree = gcal-tree (2000 + dk-bcd yy) (dk-bcd mm) (dk-bcd dd)
    in let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf tree ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds
  end

 The month never repaints, so the cursor is the only thing that moves here
 and it needs no hide-repaint-update bracket: `cursor-update` restores what
 it covered before drawing itself at the new place.

  desk-cal-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-cal-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    p0 <- desk-cal-paint base w h stride font tf ds
    let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-cal
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Issues

 The table is laid out once to be MEASURED and then rebuilt to the height
 the layout engine gave it (WORKS-23). The alternative is deriving that
 height here from the title, the two separators and the help line, which is
 a second copy of an arithmetic the engine already did and would drift from
 it the first time a pane gains a row. The measuring pass is bracketed and
 dropped, so what it costs is one layout of a tree this pane rebuilds every
 paint anyway.

 WORKS-47 wants the table's width from the same pass, so what comes back is
 the measured node's rect rather than one of its numbers. A second bracketed
 layout to read the other number would double what this already costs.

  desk-trk-table-box : Integer, Integer, DbServer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> LayoutRect
  desk-trk-table-box (w) (h) (srv) (filter) (sel) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let measured = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (gtk-tree srv filter sel (0 - 1) 0) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let found = widget-find measured "issues"
    in let box = when found
      is Just (n) -> layout-rect 0 0 ((n.wn-bounds).lr-w) ((n.wn-bounds).lr-h)
      is None -> layout-rect-zero
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in box

 WORKS-13. The same bracket-and-read-an-integer shape the desk uses
 everywhere else: lay the tree out again inside a heap bracket, ask the
 table which row the hit id names, drop the tree and keep the number. The
 tree is built at the height the paint used, so a click answers about the
 page the reader is looking at rather than about a table nobody drew.

  desk-trk-hit : Integer, Integer, UsbMouse, Integer, DbServer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  desk-trk-hit (w) (h) (mouse) (ds) (srv) (filter) (sel) (tf) =
    let box = desk-trk-table-box w h srv filter sel ds tf
    in let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (gtk-tree srv filter sel (box.lr-h) (box.lr-w)) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let row = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x (mouse-x mouse) s) (comp-hit-y (mouse-y mouse) s)
      is Just (wgt) -> gtk-row-at srv filter sel (box.lr-h) (box.lr-w) (wgt.wn-id)
      is None -> 0 - 1
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in row

  desk-trk-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, DbServer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-trk-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (srv) (filter) (sel) (ds) =
    let box = desk-trk-table-box w h srv filter sel ds tf
    in let tree = gtk-tree srv filter sel (box.lr-h) (box.lr-w)
    in let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf tree ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

  desk-trk-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, DbServer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-trk-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (srv) (filter) (sel) (ds) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-trk-draw base w h stride font tf srv filter sel ds

  desk-trk-rows : DbServer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-trk-rows (srv) (filter) =
    let r = tk-db-all-issues srv
    in list-length (gtk-rows-from-db (r.qr-rows) filter 0 (r.qr-count) [])

 The tracker database was a `let` above the loop, which a step has nowhere to
 put, and `DbServer` is a record so a `ds` cell cannot hold it. It is rebuilt
 instead, and only when there is a key to answer. That is CHEAPER than the loop
 it replaces, not dearer: the old loop ran `desk-trk-rows` on every iteration,
 which is a query and a list build per poll, to compute a clamp it usually did
 not use. The step does nothing at all until a key arrives, and a key arrives
 at human speed. `tk-init-tracker-db` is pure construction from literals, so
 rebuilding it is safe as well as affordable.

 Scancode 33 is `f`, which opens Files from the desk and cycles the filter
 here. A focused step gets the key and the dispatch does not see it, which is
 what makes a pane able to own a letter the desk also uses.

  desk-trk-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-trk-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) =
    if sc == 0 then 1
    else if sc == 1 then 0
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let srv = tk-init-tracker-db
      in let filter = peek-32 ds 32
      in let sel = peek-32 ds 40
      in let n = desk-trk-rows srv filter
      in let hi = if n > 0 then n - 1 else 0
      in let nf = if sc == 33 then int-mod (filter + 1) 3 else filter
      in let ns0 = if sc == 72 then (if sel > 0 then sel - 1 else 0) else sel
      in let ns1 = if sc == 80 then (if ns0 < hi then ns0 + 1 else hi) else ns0
      in let ns2 = if sc == 33 then 0 else ns1
      in let hit-row = if clicked then desk-trk-hit w h mouse ds srv filter sel tf else 0 - 1
      in let ns = if hit-row >= 0 then hit-row else ns2
      in if nf == filter & ns == sel then
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      else
        let f1 = poke-32 ds 32 nf
        in let s1 = poke-32 ds 40 ns
        in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
        in let p = desk-trk-draw base w h stride font tf srv nf ns ds
        in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1

  desk-trk-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-trk-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let srv = tk-init-tracker-db
    in let p0 = desk-trk-paint base w h stride font tf srv (peek-32 ds 32) (peek-32 ds 40) ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-trk
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Review

 What a pane gets for itself, in the layout's own units: the glass height
 divided by the widget scale, less the top gap `desk-chrome-with` inserts and
 the taskbar band it puts underneath. A pane that sizes anything to the space
 it has needs this, and it cannot compute it from inside because these two
 constants live here and `GopDesk` cites the panes rather than the reverse.

  desk-content-h : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  desk-content-h (w) (h) =
    h / ui-wscale w - dk-bar-h * ui-scale w / ui-wscale w - dk-task-h

 The width a wrapped line has to fit is measured rather than computed, the way
 the Issues table's height is (WORKS-23). Deriving it here means subtracting the
 sidebar, the panel padding and the label's own padding by hand, and the padding
 is a THEME's choice: `edges-uniform 4` in one scheme, `edges-xy 16 8` in
 another, so an arithmetic that agreed with the engine under the default would
 wrap at the wrong column under `lcars`. The measuring pass lays the detail out
 with wrapping off, reads the rect the description label was given, and drops
 the tree. Width does not depend on the wrap, because a column gives every child
 the container's width whatever its height, so one pass settles it and there is
 no second round.

 What comes back is a rect rather than one number because the detail needs
 both: the width a wrapped line has to fit, and the room left under it. Reading
 the second with a second bracketed layout would double what this costs.

 The rect is built AFTER the restore, from two integers the tree gave up before
 it. Building it inside the bracket and returning it hands back a record the
 restore has already released, which reads correctly only until the next
 allocation lands on it.

  desk-rev-box : Integer, Integer, GrvData, Integer, Maybe Text, Integer, GopFont -> LayoutRect
  desk-rev-box (w) (h) (data) (rs) (signer) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let measured = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (grv-tree data rs signer (desk-content-h w h) (0 - 1) 0) w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let bw = when widget-find measured "grv-body-0"
      is Just (n) -> (n.wn-bounds).lr-w
      is None -> 0 - 1
    in let sh = when widget-find measured "grv-fill"
      is Just (n) -> (n.wn-bounds).lr-h
      is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in layout-rect 0 0 bw sh

  desk-rev-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, GrvData, Integer, Integer, Maybe Text -> Integer
  desk-rev-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (data) (rs) (ds) (signer) =
    let box = if grv-in-detail rs then desk-rev-box w h data rs signer ds tf else layout-rect-zero
    in let view = if box.lr-h <= 0 then 1 else 1 + (box.lr-h) / grv-row-h
    in let tree = grv-tree data rs signer (desk-content-h w h) (box.lr-w) (if grv-in-detail rs then view else 0)
    in let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf tree ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

  desk-rev-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, GrvData, Integer, Integer, Maybe Text -> Integer
  desk-rev-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (data) (rs) (ds) (signer) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in desk-rev-draw base w h stride font tf data rs ds signer

  desk-rev-stamp : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-rev-stamp (dummy) = act
    yy <- rtc-read 9
    mm <- rtc-read 8
    dd <- rtc-read 7
    hh <- rtc-read 4
    mi <- rtc-read 2
    ss <- rtc-read 0
    let day = ((2000 + dk-bcd yy) * 100 + dk-bcd mm) * 100 + dk-bcd dd
    in (day * 100 + dk-bcd hh) * 10000 + dk-bcd mi * 100 + dk-bcd ss
  end

  desk-rev-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Identity] Integer
  desk-rev-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) =
    if sc == 0 then 1
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let rs = peek-32 ds desk-review-cell
      in let md = grv-shift-of (peek-32 rs grv-mods-cell) sc
      in let sm = poke-32 rs grv-mods-cell md
      in act
       data <- grv-load 0
       signer0 <- loaded-fingerprint
       let can = when signer0
         is None -> 0
         is Just (fp) -> 1
       in let r0 = grv-key rs sc (grv-count data) md can
       in act
        cast <- if r0 == 6 then act
            ts <- desk-rev-stamp 0
            grv-supersede data rs ts
          end else if r0 >= 3 then act
            ts <- desk-rev-stamp 0
            grv-cast data rs r0 ts
          end else 0
        let r = if r0 >= 3 then 1 else r0
        in if r == 2 then
          let m = grv-set-mode rs grv-mode-list
          in let s = grv-set-sel rs 0 1
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 0
        else if r == 0 then
          let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        else act
          data2 <- grv-reload cast data
          let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
          in let p = desk-rev-paint base w h stride font tf data2 rs ds signer0
          in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
          in let d = __heap-restore mark
          in 1
        end
       end
      end
    end

  desk-rev-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-rev-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    signer <- loaded-fingerprint
    let mark = __heap-save
    in let rs = peek-32 ds desk-review-cell
    in act
     data <- grv-load 0
     let s0 = grv-set-sel rs (grv-sel rs) (grv-count data)
     in let p0 = desk-rev-paint base w h stride font tf data rs ds signer
     in let d = __heap-restore mark
     in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-rev
     in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
     in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end
  end

Section: Diffusion

  desk-dif-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  desk-dif-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf (gdf-tree 0) ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

 The pointer tracks here and no click is dispatched, which is honest rather
 than lazy: the Generate button dispatches to nothing (WORKS-3), so a click
 that appeared to arm it would be the worse outcome. Wire the click when the
 button does something. As a step that costs nothing to keep true: this pane
 takes `desk-quiet-step`, which is handed `clicked` by nobody and answers only
 Esc and F12.

  desk-dif-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-dif-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let p0 = desk-dif-paint base w h stride font tf ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-dif
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Appearance Pane

  dk-style-tree : Integer, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dk-style-tree (scheme) (f) (snd) =
    widget-panel "style" DirColumn 4 [widget-label "style-title" "APPEARANCE", widget-button "style-scheme" ("Colour scheme: " & dk-scheme-name scheme), widget-button "style-border" ("Borders: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-border), widget-button "style-grad" ("Gradients: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-gradient), widget-button "style-shadow" ("Shadows: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-shadow), widget-button "style-round" ("Rounded corners: " & dk-on-off f dk-adorn-round), widget-button "style-sound" ("Sounds: " & dk-sound-name snd), widget-label "style-hint" "Click a row, or space b g s r a. Esc returns.", widget-set-flex (widget-label "style-spacer" "") 1]

  dk-sound-name : Integer -> Text
  dk-sound-name (v) = if v == 0 then "off" else "on"

  dk-style-id-code : Text -> Integer
  dk-style-id-code (id) =
    if id == "style-scheme" then 1
    else if id == "style-border" then 2
    else if id == "style-grad" then 3
    else if id == "style-shadow" then 4
    else if id == "style-round" then 5
    else if id == "style-sound" then 6
    else 0

  dk-style-key : Integer -> Integer
  dk-style-key (sc) =
    if sc == 57 then 1
    else if sc == 48 then 2
    else if sc == 34 then 3
    else if sc == 31 then 4
    else if sc == 19 then 5
    else if sc == 30 then 6
    else 0

  dk-style-hit : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont -> Integer
  dk-style-hit (w) (h) (mx) (my) (ds) (tf) =
    let probe = __heap-save
    in let tree = dk-style-tree (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell)
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face tree w ds tf) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let code = when ev-hit-widget laid (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
          is Just (wgt) -> dk-style-id-code (wgt.wn-id)
          is None -> 0
    in let dropped = __heap-restore probe
    in code

  dk-style-flip : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-style-flip (ds) (bit) = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell (dk-adorn-flip (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell) bit)

  dk-style-apply : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dk-style-apply (ds) (code) =
    if code == 1 then poke-32 ds dk-scheme-cell (int-mod (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell + 1) dk-scheme-count)
    else if code == 2 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-border
    else if code == 3 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-gradient
    else if code == 4 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-shadow
    else if code == 5 then dk-style-flip ds dk-adorn-round
    else if code == 6 then poke-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell (1 - peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell)
    else 0

  desk-style-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, Integer -> Integer
  desk-style-paint (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (ds) =
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h (dk-wall ds)
    in let tree = dk-style-tree (peek-32 ds dk-scheme-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-adorn-cell) (peek-32 ds dk-sound-on-cell)
    in let laid = dk-chrome-paint base w h stride font tf tree ds
    in desk-topbar base w stride font (ui-scale w) ds

 The first step that needs the POINTER as well as the key, which is why the
 protocol carries the whole event: `desk-step-of` takes `sc` and `clicked`, and
 a pane that only reads keys ignores the second. Appearance is driven from
 either, and `dk-style-key` and `dk-style-hit` already fold both into one code,
 which is the same "one road" the launcher rule asks for.

 All of its state is `dk-scheme-cell` and `dk-adorn-cell`, both integers, so
 nothing here has to survive in a pointer and the step keeps no state of its
 own at all.

  desk-style-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-style-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (sc) (clicked) =
    let click = if clicked then dk-style-hit w h (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) ds tf else 0
    in let code = if click /= 0 then click else dk-style-key sc
    in if sc == 1 then 0
    else if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        s <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if code == 0 then 1
    else
      let mark = __heap-save
      in let a = dk-style-apply ds code
      in act
        sv <- dk-settings-save ds
        let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
        in let p = desk-style-paint base w h stride font tf ds
        in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end

  desk-style-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-style-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let p0 = desk-style-paint base w h stride font tf ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-style
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Loop

  desk-widget-hit : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  desk-widget-hit (root) (mx) (my) (id) (s) =
    when ev-hit-widget root (comp-hit-x mx s) (comp-hit-y my s)
      is Just (wgt) -> wgt.wn-id == id
      is None -> False

 Every pane hid the cursor on entry and left it hidden, so the arrow died
 the moment a pane opened and came back only on the way out. These two put
 it back on the desktop's own terms: `dk-cursor` is the paint, and `dk-pump`
 is the poll that answers whether the pointer moved.

 The panes do not need the browser's memoized tree. A pane's state lives in
 `ds` cells written with `poke-32` rather than on the heap, so a hit test can
 lay the tree out again inside a bracket, read an INTEGER out of it, and drop
 the whole thing -- there is no state allocated above the tree that a release
 would take with it. `widget-layout` on the same tree the paint hands
 `comp-render` gives the same bounds, because comp-render lays out exactly
 that and nothing else.

  dk-cursor : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbMouse -> Integer
  dk-cursor (base) (w) (h) (stride) (cs) (mouse) =
    if mouse.um-ok
    then cursor-update cs base stride w h (ui-scale w) (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse)
    else 0

  dk-pump : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, UsbMouse -> Integer
  dk-pump (base) (w) (h) (stride) (cs) (mouse) =
    if mouse-pump mouse w h == 1
    then let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse in 1
    else 0

Section: The Editor

 Its 9 MB buffer is allocated lazily by `ged-ensure` and parked in
 `desk-edit-cell`, and handed to the step as `es`. Only its typed values ride
 in `EditState`.

 **This block said the base mark cannot reach that buffer. It can, and this is
 the correction.** `ged-init` runs from `desk-edit-open`, which is ABOVE the
 base mark, so a close restores over it. `ged-ensure` (`GopEdit.codex:49`) only
 allocates when the pointer is zero, so the next open would reuse an address
 the allocator has already handed out again. Measured 2026-08-19: an Edit pane
 alive and hidden holds 9,598,200 bytes, and after it closes the frontier is
 72 bytes over a desk that never opened one, so the 9,437,184 that `ged-init`
 allocates is demonstrably reclaimed. Both close paths therefore zero the
 pointer whenever the restore target is at or below it, which costs one
 re-allocation per Edit session and removes the dangling reuse.

 It was two loops, a file list with the editor nested inside it, and a step
 cannot nest. They are one step over a mode now, and leaving the editor is a
 mode change rather than a stack unwind.

  desk-edit-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, Integer, Boolean -> [Identity, Device.Port] Integer
  desk-edit-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (sc) (clicked) =
    when apps.da-edit
      is None -> 0
      is Just (st) -> act
        r <- ged-step base w h stride font (dk-pal ds) mouse cs (peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell) st sc clicked
        if r == ged-step-hide then desk-step-hide else r
      end

  desk-edit-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-edit-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    when apps.da-edit
      is Just (st) -> desk-edit-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root st
      is None -> desk-edit-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root

  desk-edit-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, EditState -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-edit-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (st) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let root2 = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let mk = __heap-save
    in let p = ged-reenter base w h stride font (dk-pal ds) mouse cs (peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell) st
    in let dr = __heap-restore mk
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-edit
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root2

  desk-edit-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-edit-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    let ke = desk-browser-evict ds
    in let mp = desk-marks-push (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-edit __heap-save
    in act
    st <- ged-new base w h stride (ui-scale w) font (dk-pal ds) mouse cs (peek-32 ds desk-edit-cell)
    let apps2 = DeskApps { da-files = apps.da-files, da-browser = None, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = Just st }
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-edit
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps2 root
  end

Section: Files

 Files is the first pane whose state the desk carries in the typed channel
 rather than in a `ds` cell. A mounted volume, a directory listing, the
 cluster stack and the names down to it are records and lists, and a cell
 holds 32 bits. `GopFiles` still owns every measure and every pixel; what
 moves here is only the loop.

 The state is allocated by `desk-files-open`, which runs after `desk-run`
 took the base mark and before `desk-loop` takes its frame mark. That places
 it above the one and below the other, so the per-iteration restore never
 reaches it and the close restore always does. Moving the selection stores
 an INTEGER into the record in place, which is safe across a restore however
 the record's other fields were allocated. Changing directory stores new
 LISTS, which is not, and that is what the negative answer is for.

  desk-files-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-files-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    when apps.da-files
      is Just (st) -> desk-files-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root st
      is None -> desk-files-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root

  desk-files-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, FilesState -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-files-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (st) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let mk = __heap-save
    in act
      hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
      let d = gfl-draw-list base w h stride s font (dk-pal ds) (st.fs-entries) (st.fs-sel) (st.fs-top) (st.fs-names)
      in let dr = __heap-restore mk
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-files
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    end

  desk-files-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-files-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    let ke = desk-browser-evict ds
    in let mp = desk-marks-push (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-files __heap-save
    in act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let s = ui-scale w
    in if m.gm-ok == False then act
      f <- gfl-draw-frame base w h stride s font (dk-pal ds) "Files"
      t <- gop-draw-text base stride font (gfl-px w s + 10 * s) (gfl-py h s + 26 * s) (shot-diag-text "no FAT ESP on the boot medium") (gfl-dim (dk-pal ds)) s 0
      t2 <- gop-draw-text base stride font (gfl-px w s + 10 * s) (gfl-py h s + gfl-ph h s - 18 * s) "Esc back" (gfl-hint (dk-pal ds)) s 0
      let st = FilesState { fs-vol = m.gm-vol, fs-entries = [], fs-clusters = [0 - 1], fs-names = [], fs-sel = 0, fs-top = 0, fs-ptr = 0, fs-mode = 0 }
      in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-files
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (DeskApps { da-files = Just st, da-browser = None, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = apps.da-edit }) root
    end
    else act
      let flag = if gfat-is32 (m.gm-vol) then (m.gm-vol).vol-root-start else 0 - 1
      in let clusters = [flag]
      in act
        ents <- gfl-list-cur (m.gm-vol) clusters
        let st = FilesState { fs-vol = m.gm-vol, fs-entries = ents, fs-clusters = clusters, fs-names = [], fs-sel = 0, fs-top = 0, fs-ptr = 0, fs-mode = 0 }
        in let d = gfl-draw-list base w h stride s font (dk-pal ds) ents 0 0 (st.fs-names)
        in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-files
        in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
        in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (DeskApps { da-files = Just st, da-browser = None, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = apps.da-edit }) root
      end
    end
  end

  desk-files-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (sc) (clicked) =
    when apps.da-files
      is None -> 0
      is Just (st) -> desk-files-on base w h stride font kbd mouse ds cs st sc clicked

 The pointer position the pane last acted on is a field rather than a `ds`
 cell because it is the pane's, and the record is where the pane's state now
 lives. It is what stands in for the `mv` flag the old loop got from
 `mouse-pump`: without it, a pointer resting over a row would drag the
 selection back on every poll and the arrow keys would not work.

  desk-files-moved : FilesState, UsbMouse -> Boolean
  desk-files-moved (st) (mouse) = st.fs-ptr /= (mouse-x mouse) * 4096 + (mouse-y mouse)

  desk-files-on : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-on (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (st) (sc) (clicked) =
    if st.fs-mode == 1 then desk-files-viewing base w h stride font mouse ds cs st sc clicked
    else desk-files-listing base w h stride font kbd mouse ds cs st sc clicked

 A previewed file is a still picture, so this is the shared quiet step's shape
 rather than a mode with machinery: answer Esc and the close box by repainting
 the list, answer F12, and answer everything else by doing nothing. The preview
 keeps no state at all beyond the mode integer, because nothing repaints it --
 the only band the desk redraws on the second is the taskbar, and the Files
 panel ends above it.

  desk-files-viewing : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-viewing (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (st) (sc) (clicked) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 1 | sc == 28 | (clicked & gfl-close-hit w h s (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse)) then
      let a = __record-set st "fs-mode" 0
      in let b = __record-set st "fs-ptr" ((mouse-x mouse) * 4096 + (mouse-y mouse))
      in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
      in let p = gfl-draw-list base w h stride s font (dk-pal ds) (st.fs-entries) (st.fs-sel) (st.fs-top) (st.fs-names)
      in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in 1
    else 1

  desk-files-listing : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-listing (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (st) (sc) (clicked) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let n = list-length (st.fs-entries)
    in let sel = st.fs-sel
    in let mv = desk-files-moved st mouse
    in let hover = if mv | clicked then gfl-row-at w h s (st.fs-top) n (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) else 0 - 1
    in let pk = __record-set st "fs-ptr" ((mouse-x mouse) * 4096 + (mouse-y mouse))
    in if sc == 88 then act
      let mark = __heap-save
      in act
        r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
        let d = __heap-restore mark
        in 1
      end
    end
    else if sc == 15 then desk-step-hide
    else if sc == 1 | (clicked & gfl-close-hit w h s (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse)) then
      if list-length (st.fs-clusters) <= 1 then 0
      else desk-files-up base w h stride font mouse cs st ds
    else if (sc == 28 & n > 0) | (clicked & hover >= 0) then
      desk-files-pick base w h stride font kbd mouse cs st (if clicked & hover >= 0 then hover else sel) ds
    else if sc == 72 & sel > 0 then desk-files-move base w h stride font mouse cs st (sel - 1) ds
    else if sc == 80 & sel < n - 1 then desk-files-move base w h stride font mouse cs st (sel + 1) ds
    else if hover >= 0 & hover /= sel then desk-files-move base w h stride font mouse cs st hover ds
    else 1

  desk-files-move : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-move (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (mouse) (cs) (st) (ns) (ds) =
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let t2 = gfl-clamp-top ns (st.fs-top) (gfl-rows-vis h s)
    in let a = __record-set st "fs-sel" ns
    in let b = __record-set st "fs-top" t2
    in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = gfl-draw-list base w h stride s font (dk-pal ds) (st.fs-entries) ns t2 (st.fs-names)
    in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in 1

 Opening a file paints the preview and takes a mode. It used to call
 `GopFiles`' modal wait loop, which stopped the desk clock for as long as the
 file was on screen -- the last place in the desk where that was still true
 (WORKS-39). The preview keeps nothing: its sector buffer and its text are
 allocated inside the frame and answering 1 frees them, because no later
 iteration reads them again.

  desk-files-pick : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-pick (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (mouse) (cs) (st) (idx) (ds) =
    let e = list-at (st.fs-entries) idx
    in if gfl-is-dir e then desk-files-enter base w h stride font mouse cs st e ds
    else act
      p <- gfl-preview-paint base w h stride (ui-scale w) font (dk-pal ds) cs (st.fs-vol) e
      let a = __record-set st "fs-sel" idx
      in let b = __record-set st "fs-top" (gfl-clamp-top idx (st.fs-top) (gfl-rows-vis h (ui-scale w)))
      in let m = __record-set st "fs-mode" 1
      in let c = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in 1
    end

  desk-files-enter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Fat16DirEntry, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-enter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (mouse) (cs) (st) (e) (ds) = act
    let c2 = list-push (st.fs-clusters) (e.de-cluster)
    in let n2 = list-push (st.fs-names) (e.de-name)
    in act
      ents <- gfl-list-cur (st.fs-vol) c2
      desk-files-land base w h stride font mouse cs st ents c2 n2 ds
    end
  end

  desk-files-up : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-up (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (mouse) (cs) (st) (ds) = act
    let c2 = gfl-pop-int (st.fs-clusters)
    in let n2 = gfl-pop-text (st.fs-names)
    in act
      ents <- gfl-list-cur (st.fs-vol) c2
      desk-files-land base w h stride font mouse cs st ents c2 n2 ds
    end
  end

 The three list stores happen together and the answer is negative, so the
 frame that holds them is kept. Answering 1 here would free the listing the
 record now points at and the next repaint would walk freed memory.

  desk-files-land : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbMouse, Integer, FilesState, List Fat16DirEntry, List Integer, List Text, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-files-land (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (mouse) (cs) (st) (ents) (c2) (n2) (ds) =
    let a = __record-set st "fs-clusters" c2
    in let b = __record-set st "fs-names" n2
    in let c = __record-set st "fs-entries" ents
    in let d = __record-set st "fs-sel" 0
    in let f = __record-set st "fs-top" 0
    in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let p = gfl-draw-list base w h stride (ui-scale w) font (dk-pal ds) ents 0 0 n2
    in let cu = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in 0 - 1

 The close bracket is not decoration and it now matters more, not less.
 `gsc-new` allocates the render target and its depth buffer outside any extent
 of its own, measured at 4,617,256 bytes for one visit at 1024x768 and the same
 again for the next, and the boot image gives Codex a 128 MB heap
 (`build-option-a.ps1 -AllocPages 32768`, which is `cdx-to-pe`'s
 `-HeapPages`) rather than the ~3 GB a codex-vm bed hands out. Nothing inside
 the pane releases it; `desk-app-close` restoring to the base mark is what does,
 on every exit, and without that every trip into the pane spends the arena the
 shot path's sector buffers are drawn from, permanently, and no bed at the
 default memory size can express the cost.

Section: The 3D panes

 One step and one record serve both, because `GopScene` and `GopFish` always
 shared the loop. What separates them here is the two focus ids and the scene
 builder each passes, which is where the difference already lived.

 This pane is the one whose per-iteration cost is a FRAME rather than a poll,
 so the desk's loop turns over at the frame rate: about 16 ms in the bed on the
 host rasterizer, about 86 to 135 ms on the software path, and near a second on
 metal. The clock still advances, just no finer than a frame. Nothing here
 keeps a frame -- the toggles and the counter are integers stored into the
 record in place, and everything a frame builds is freed by `desk-loop`'s own
 bracket, which is the job `gsc-loop`'s mark used to do.

  desk-scene-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, DeskApps, (Integer -> Scene3DState), Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (mouse) (cs) (apps) (build) (sc) =
    when apps.da-scene
      is None -> 0
      is Just (sp) -> act
        r <- gsc-step base w h stride font kbd mouse cs build sp sc
        if r == gsc-step-hide then desk-step-hide else r
      end

  desk-scene-sibling : Integer -> Integer
  desk-scene-sibling (foc) = if foc == desk-focus-scene then desk-focus-fish else desk-focus-scene

  desk-scene-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sky) (foc) =
    let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let live = desk-marks-find ms foc (desk-marks-depth ms - 1) >= 0
    in if live then desk-scene-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root foc
    else desk-scene-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root sky foc

  desk-scene-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (foc) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let root2 = desk-draw base w h stride font (ui-scale w) tf ds
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell foc
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root2

  desk-scene-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-scene-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sky) (foc) =
    let ms = peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell
    in let ko = desk-marks-kill ms (desk-scene-sibling foc)
    in let ke = desk-browser-evict ds
    in let mp = desk-marks-push ms foc __heap-save
    in act
    sp <- gsc-new base w h stride cs (dk-gpu ds) sky
    let apps2 = DeskApps { da-files = apps.da-files, da-browser = None, da-scene = Just sp, da-edit = apps.da-edit }
    in let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell foc
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps2 root
  end


 F12 writes the live frame to the boot stick as SHhhmmss.BMP and paints
 the verdict into the taskbar -- the telemetry channel that retires the
 camera rig. The whole capture is heap-bracketed: the 2-and-a-bit
 megabyte frame copy and the FAT image are reclaimed before the loop
 continues, after the verdict Text has been painted.

  desk-shot : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-shot (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (ds) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      t <- shot-take base w h stride
      let pal = dk-pal ds
      in let s = ui-scale w
      in let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride (200 * s) (h - dk-task-h * s + 4 * s) (300 * s) (16 * s) (pal.pal-muted)
      in let tx = gop-draw-text base stride font (204 * s) (h - dk-task-h * s + 6 * s) t (pal.pal-primary) s 0
      in let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 0
    end
  end

  desk-snap : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-snap (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
    desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

  desk-shutdown : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-shutdown (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    r <- acpi-poweroff (acpi-boot 0)
    let s = dk-win-s w h tf
    in let m = desk-line base stride h font s tf (dk-win-x w s + 10 * s) (dk-win-y h s tf + (dk-win-h tf s - 24) * s) "firmware declined the power transition" ((dk-pal ds).pal-error)
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

Section: Browser

 The Browser is the one pane that covers the desk instead of floating over it,
 and it stays that way here. Its click handling tests absolute Y against
 `chrome-tab-bar-height` and `chrome-height`, so its tree has to start at the
 top of the framebuffer; composing it into the content slot beside the sidebar
 would move every hit by the sidebar's width and the top bar's height, which is
 a change to `Browser.codex`'s coordinate model and not to this pane.

 What it does give back is the taskbar strip, and the height is MEASURED from
 the laid-out band rather than computed from `dk-task-h`. The arithmetic
 assumed the band sits flush with the bottom of the screen and it does not:
 the root panel's own padding leaves 8 logical rows below it, which
 `desk-taskbar-hit.expected` has recorded since it was written. At 1600x900
 that arithmetic answered 844 where the band begins at 828, so the pane was
 granted sixteen rows of the band and painted a page into them on every
 repaint. Measured 2026-08-20 against the goldens: rows 828 to 843 carried
 page content after a dispatched key and the band's gradient before it.

 A tree with no taskbar falls back to the old arithmetic, which is what the
 standalone browser and any bed that renders this chrome without a band get.

  desk-bro-h : Integer, Integer, WidgetNode -> Integer
  desk-bro-h (w) (h) (root) =
    when widget-find root "taskbar"
      is None -> h - dk-task-h * ui-wscale w
      is Just (tb) ->
        let b = tb.wn-bounds
        in b.lr-y * ui-wscale w

 **Why a heavy open EVICTS a live Browser, when it does not evict Files or the
 3D panes.** `gbr-repaint` takes `bs-laid-mark` at the CURRENT frontier and
 rebuilds `bp-st` there, so a Browser re-entered above another pane has its
 state above THAT pane's mark. When the pane below closes and the cascade pops
 to its mark, the Browser's state goes with it and the record still points at
 it. Files and the 3D panes cannot do this: their state is built once by their
 own `-open`, above their own mark and below everything later. So the rule is
 that the Browser may only ever be the topmost heavy pane, and every other
 heavy `-open` kills its entry and clears its field. A LIGHT pane opening over
 it is safe and is the case this buys: a light close finds the Browser's entry
 live on top, reclaims nothing, and the Browser's marks are untouched.

  desk-browser-evict : Integer -> Integer
  desk-browser-evict (ds) = desk-marks-kill (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-bro

  desk-browser-focus : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-browser-focus (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    when apps.da-browser
      is Just (bp) -> desk-browser-reenter base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root bp
      is None -> desk-browser-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root

  desk-browser-reenter : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, BrowserPane -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-browser-reenter (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (bp) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    p <- gbr-first-paint base w (desk-bro-h w h root) stride font tf mouse cs (ui-scale w) bp
    let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-bro
    in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
    in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

  desk-browser-open : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-browser-open (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) =
    let mp = desk-marks-push (peek-32 ds desk-marks-cell) desk-focus-bro __heap-save
    in act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    bp <- gbr-new w (desk-bro-h w h root)
    let apps2 = DeskApps { da-files = apps.da-files, da-browser = Just bp, da-scene = apps.da-scene, da-edit = apps.da-edit }
    in act
      p <- gbr-first-paint base w (desk-bro-h w h root) stride font tf mouse cs (ui-scale w) bp
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-bro
      in let inval = poke-32 ds desk-second-cell (0 - 1)
      in let c1 = dk-cursor base w h stride cs mouse
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps2 root
    end
  end

  desk-browser-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  desk-browser-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sc) (clicked) =
    when apps.da-browser
      is None -> 0
      is Just (bp) ->
        if sc == 1 then 0
        else if sc == 15 then desk-step-hide
        else if sc == 88 then act
          let mark = __heap-save
          in act
            r <- desk-shot base w h stride font ds
            let d = __heap-restore mark
            in 1
          end
        end
        else gbr-step base w (desk-bro-h w h root) stride font tf mouse cs (ui-scale w) bp sc clicked

 The dispatch is separate from the polling so that the launcher can reach
 it. A pane is chosen by a scancode, and `desk-programs` answers one, so a
 row in the launcher and the key it names arrive here by the same road
 rather than through a second table that has to agree with this one.

 The idle case does NOT go through `desk-dispatch`, and that is load
 bearing rather than an optimisation. This loop polls continuously, and
 routing the idle iteration through a second function makes the recursion
 mutual instead of self, which stops being a tail call: the stack then
 grows once per poll and the desk double-faults (`!EXC=08`, CR2 beside
 RSP) within seconds of boot. Measured, by writing it the other way
 first. Only an actual keystroke or click may cross into the dispatch.

 A step answers 0 to close, 1 to stay open, a NEGATIVE number to stay open
 without the frame restore, and anything else as a scancode to dispatch after
 closing. The scancode arm is unambiguous because every dispatch scancode is
 at least 2.

 The negative answer exists for the one thing the frame bracket cannot serve:
 a step that GREW its own durable state this iteration. The bracket frees
 everything a step allocated, which is what keeps an idle desk flat, and a
 pane like Files that swapped its directory listing for a new one needs that
 listing to survive into the next poll. It cannot be moved below the frame
 mark after the fact, because a bump allocator has save and restore and
 nothing else. So the step keeps the frame instead, and pays for the
 iteration's transient allocations along with the state it meant to keep.
 That is a real cost and it is bounded by how often a human changes
 directory, not by the poll rate. Only a step that actually grew state may
 answer negative; answering it on a repaint would put the idle leak back.

  desk-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-loop (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) = act
    let fmark = __heap-save
    in act
      sc <- kbd-take kbd
      ck <- desk-clock base w h stride font tf ds root
      let mv = mouse-pump mouse w h
      in let cu = if mv == 1 then cursor-update cs base stride w h (ui-scale w) (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) else 0
      in let clicked = mv == 1 & mouse-clicked mouse
      in act
        cl <- dk-snd-click ds clicked
        if peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell /= desk-focus-none then act
          alive <- desk-step-of base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root (peek-32 ds desk-focus-cell) sc clicked
          if alive == 0 then desk-app-close base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps
          else if alive == desk-step-hide then desk-app-hide base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps
          else if alive == 1 then
            let d = __heap-restore fmark
            in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
          else if alive < 0 then desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
          else desk-app-close-to base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps alive
        end
        else if sc == 0 & clicked == False then
          let d = __heap-restore fmark
          in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
        else
          let d = __heap-restore fmark
          in desk-dispatch base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root sc clicked
      end
    end
  end

  desk-dispatch : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, DeskApps, WidgetNode, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
  desk-dispatch (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (kbd) (mouse) (ds) (cs) (apps) (root) (sc) (clicked) =
    let files = if sc == 33 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "files" (ui-wscale w)
    in let scene = (if sc == 4 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "scene" (ui-wscale w))
    in let fish = (if sc == 16 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "fish" (ui-wscale w))
    in let mon = if sc == 50 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "monitor" (ui-wscale w)
    in let calc = if sc == 46 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "calc" (ui-wscale w)
    in let con = if sc == 20 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "console" (ui-wscale w)
    in let cal = if sc == 38 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "cal" (ui-wscale w)
    in let trk = if sc == 23 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "issues" (ui-wscale w)
    in let rev = if sc == 19 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "review" (ui-wscale w)
    in let dif = if sc == 32 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "diffusion" (ui-wscale w)
    in let bro = if sc == 48 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "browser" (ui-wscale w)
    in let edt = if sc == 18 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "edit" (ui-wscale w)
    in let sty = if sc == 30 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "style" (ui-wscale w)
    in let bye = clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "shutdown" (ui-wscale w)
    in let prg = if sc == 25 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "programs" (ui-wscale w)
    in let sysm = if sc == 41 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "menu" (ui-wscale w)
    in let clk = if sc == 37 then True else clicked & desk-widget-hit root (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) "clock" (ui-wscale w)
    in if sc == 88 then desk-snap base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if sysm then desk-menu-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) apps root
    else if prg then desk-programs-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) apps root
    else if clk then desk-clock-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell) apps root
    else if files then desk-files-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if scene then desk-scene-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root gsc-sky desk-focus-scene
    else if fish then desk-scene-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root gf-sky desk-focus-fish
    else if mon then
      let sf = poke-32 ds desk-focus-cell desk-focus-mon
      in let due = poke-32 ds 16 255
      in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if calc then desk-calc-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds 24) apps root
    else if con then desk-console-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs (peek-32 ds desk-console-cell) apps root
    else if cal then desk-cal-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if trk then desk-trk-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if rev then desk-rev-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if dif then desk-dif-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if bro then desk-browser-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if edt then desk-edit-focus base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if sty then desk-style-open base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else if bye then desk-shutdown base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
    else desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end

 Loaded at the ppem the widget layer will draw it at, which is why this takes
 the display width rather than the ignored argument it used to. `gbf-put-text`
 paints glyph bitmaps at their rasterized size and takes no scale, so a face
 loaded at a fixed 16 is half-height beside scaled bitmap text at 1600 and
 above.

  desk-font : Integer -> [Device.Port] GopFont
  desk-font (w) = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then gfont-none
    else gfont-load (m.gm-vol) "CMUNSS.TTF" (comp-glyph-h * ui-wscale w)
  end

  desk-run : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory, Identity, Audio] Integer
 The calculator's state block is allocated once here and its ADDRESS is
 kept in a `ds` cell, so no pane signature has to grow a parameter. The
 cell is 32-bit: there is no `poke-qword` builtin (only `peek-qword`), and
 a heap address fits because the bare-metal arena tops out below 4 GB --
 `peek-32` zero-extends, so it round-trips exactly rather than sign-
 extending into a negative pointer.

  desk-run (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (mouse) = act
    tf <- desk-font w
    gp <- desk-gpu-present 0
    st <- gst-load (gst-store dk-settings-keys)
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let ds = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in let cs = alloc-zeroed 2048 64
    in let ks = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let ns = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let kp = poke-32 ds 24 ks
    in let np = poke-32 ds desk-console-cell ns
    in let ep = poke-32 ds desk-edit-cell (alloc-zeroed 128 64)
    in let ci = gcon-init ns
    in let pp = poke-32 ds desk-prog-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let kp2 = poke-32 ds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let rvp = poke-32 ds desk-review-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let rvi = grv-init (peek-32 ds desk-review-cell)
    in let msp = poke-32 ds desk-marks-cell (alloc-zeroed 128 64)
    in let gpk = poke-32 ds desk-gpu-cell gp
    in let sdi = dk-snd-init ds
    in let ici = dk-icons-init ds tf w
    in let ap = dk-settings-into ds st
    in let wp = mouse-warp mouse (w / 2) (h / 2)
    in let apps = desk-apps-empty
    in let bm = poke-32 ds desk-mark-cell __heap-save
    in let root = desk-draw base w h stride font s tf ds
    in let c0 = cursor-update cs base stride w h s (w / 2) (h / 2)
    in desk-loop base w h stride font tf kbd mouse ds cs apps root
  end
